WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:11.830
Good morning, everybody, and welcome to this
meeting in public of the Board of NHS England.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:14.150
We're meeting here in London.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:19.260
We have members of the public in the room
with us, and we're also, of course, live streaming

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:25.150
the meeting, entirely consistent with our
view that the public of this country are entitled

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:30.349
to see the way in which deliberations and
decisions are taken by the Board on behalf

00:00:30.349 --> 00:00:34.110
of the patients and population that we serve.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:39.969
I have no apologies for this month's meeting,
we have a full turn-out of everybody.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:46.039
I'm going to invite members of the Board to
declare any agenda items where a conflict

00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:47.039
of interest may occur.

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:52.829
Now, I preface that by saying that we have,
of course, a register of interests that everybody

00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:56.079
has, and everybody has their own entry in
that register sitting in front of them right

00:00:56.079 --> 00:00:57.079
now.

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:00.039
Please could you ensure that that's updated
regularly?

00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:05.770
But secondly, specifically in relation to
items on the agenda today, are there any interests

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.730
that people would wish to record?

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:12.810
I'm a board member of IOCOM, which is a digital
provider to the NHS.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:18.320
So, that's in relation to our item on primary
care and digital services, okay.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.890
Are there any other interests you wish me
to record?

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:25.010
And please feel free, of course, to raise
them should the conversation inadvertently

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:31.100
veer off in a direction which comes close
to colliding with an undeclared interest.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.250
Good, thank you very much.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.570
Now, we're going to have two additional attendees
this morning.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:40.450
Dominic Hardy will join us on the General
Practice Services Programme update; and Neil

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:46.780
Churchill, Director of Experience, etc., on
the People and Communities Task Force.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:52.160
One formal announcement which I always have
to make is, there is no fire alarm due - test

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.280
due during the course of the next couple of
hours, so if it does go off, our expert team

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.070
will escort everybody safely off the premises.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.690
It will not be a test.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:07.180
Right, so let's get stuck straight in to the
business of the meeting.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:11.280
The first item is the minutes of the previous
meeting.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.470
Can I just invite my colleagues first of all
to confirm or deny that these are a fair and

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:16.980
accurate summary?

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.340
You're happy with that?

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:24.170
And are there any matters arising from those
minutes which are not otherwise on the agenda

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:25.550
for today's meeting?

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:26.550
No?

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.230
Okay, thank you very much.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:31.340
Let me then go straight to the Chair's report.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:37.990
I have filed a written report, which is hope
is sufficiently comprehensive to give people

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:44.070
an idea of what's been going on over the last
few months since our last meeting, focusing

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:48.870
particularly on future funding for the NHS,
and we will no doubt be touching upon that

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.890
again this morning.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:55.290
Development of our plans for closer working
with NHS Improvement, which is an active programme,

00:02:55.290 --> 00:03:01.520
and the Board have been in strategic sessions
working very closely with the Board of NHS

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.459
Improvement and with their senior directors
to try to understand how this complex and

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:10.980
quite challenging process, which is not a
full merger, but which is nonetheless trying

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:16.200
to draw together as much of our central activities
as possible, to reduce duplication and overlap,

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:22.740
but also give a strategic lead around integration
across the whole of the Service.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:27.830
And then also, I've made mention of the roll-out
of the genomic medicine service.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:34.770
I just want to touch also on the resignation
of Paul Baumann as our Chief Financial Officer,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:43.110
which has come, I know, as great news in the
Baumann household, but not great news around

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:46.280
the Board table, and Paul, we will miss you
enormously.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.520
This is not the opportunity for us to farewell
to you because you will be with us, we hope,

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:54.410
until the end of October, but to congratulate
you on that appointment; but also, to assure

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:59.640
the Board that arrangements are now in hand
for the search for Paul's successor, which

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:03.470
is a critical appointment, particularly in
the context of what I've just been talking

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:10.280
about, which is the closer working between
NHS England and NHS Improvement.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:15.120
So, I won't say anything further on the written
report, but I would like the Board to join

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:21.950
me in congratulating those members of the
staff of NHS England upon whom honours were

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:27.840
conferred in the Queen's Birthday's honours
list at the beginning of June, and in part,

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:33.259
in recognition of this fantastic institution
called the NHS in England.

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:38.009
The names that I'll read out very briefly:
Yvonne Coghill, the Director of Workforce

00:04:38.009 --> 00:04:43.699
Race Equality Standard Implementation, who
has been awarded a CBE, an extraordinarily

00:04:43.699 --> 00:04:49.020
powerful and well-deserved award; alongside
Roger Kline, with whom she's worked very closely

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:54.800
in the past, and who has been awarded an OBE,
and both of them for their commitment for

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.110
equality and diversity in the NHS.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:04.789
Again, a particularly valuable award, I think,
to Professor Sue Hill, who's our Scientific

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:10.479
Director, who's been made a Dame Commander
of the British Empire in reflection of her

00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:13.680
outstanding leadership on the genomics programme.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:20.050
Neil Churchill, who will be with us today,
for his dedication to carers and the voluntary

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.520
sector was awarded an OBE.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:27.319
And Dr Nishma Manek, who's a trainee GP and
one of our clinical fellows, who founded the

00:05:27.319 --> 00:05:32.050
Next Generation GP programme for emerging
leaders, received a BEM.

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:37.360
So, please will you join me in recording our
congratulations and appreciation to those

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.509
who have been awarded these honours.

00:05:39.509 --> 00:05:41.610
Right, let me pause there.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:45.190
Any questions or comments on the Chair's report?

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:46.190
No?

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:47.190
Thank you.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:51.479
In which case, may I move on to the next item
of business, which is the Chief Executive's

00:05:51.479 --> 00:05:52.479
report.

00:05:52.479 --> 00:05:53.479
Simon, over to you.

00:05:53.479 --> 00:05:54.689
Thanks, Malcolm.

00:05:54.689 --> 00:05:57.469
I was just going to very briefly cover two
things.

00:05:57.469 --> 00:06:04.089
First, since we last met, we obviously have
the announcement of the additional funding

00:06:04.089 --> 00:06:10.319
for the National Health Service over the next
five years, funding which gives us certainty

00:06:10.319 --> 00:06:17.389
against which to plan, and clearly represents
a step up in the growth rate of resource that

00:06:17.389 --> 00:06:19.300
will be available.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:26.150
Over the last five years, NHS funding growth
has averaged about 2.3% a year, and over the

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:32.059
next five years it will be 3.4%, so that clearly
represents a step change.

00:06:32.059 --> 00:06:38.210
But linked to that, we are, as the Prime Minister
has set out, going to be using the next five

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:44.839
months between now and the end of November
to, with our partners, patients' groups, clinical

00:06:44.839 --> 00:06:51.620
leaders, front-line staff, chart a set of
ten-year goals for the National Health Service,

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:54.930
linked to a five-year concrete delivery plan.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:07:01.740
And as part of that, this Board is going to
have to, at the end of September meeting,

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:08.160
make some preliminary judgements about allocations
and around some of the financial rules that

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:14.229
will need to apply, not just for 19/20 but
for the years subsequent to that.

00:07:14.229 --> 00:07:20.939
And we will also be working closely with Health
Education England around the linked ten-year

00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:25.669
workforce strategy, which has got to underpin
this, as well as with colleagues in local

00:07:25.669 --> 00:07:33.499
government and PHE on the question of social
care and prevention, which is integral.

00:07:33.499 --> 00:07:39.809
We expect to be able to, with the Department
of Health, make some further capital prioritisation

00:07:39.809 --> 00:07:48.610
decisions between now and our next public
meeting, but the larger question of the capital

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:55.929
looking out over ten years will also be linked
to the ten-year plan and the budget and spending

00:07:55.929 --> 00:07:58.569
review process that the government will undertake.

00:07:58.569 --> 00:08:04.319
But I just want to record my thanks to all
of the people in NHS England who worked very

00:08:04.319 --> 00:08:09.939
diligently to help secure that settlement.

00:08:09.939 --> 00:08:15.449
And more generally, the second point to make,
Malcolm, is that as everybody knows, tomorrow

00:08:15.449 --> 00:08:24.490
the NHS celebrates its 70th anniversary, and
that's an opportunity to both look back at

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:31.930
all of the changes that have occurred, not
with a sense of complacency, but with a sense

00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:33.090
of legitimate achievement.

00:08:33.090 --> 00:08:40.029
And also, frankly, a chance to thank the staff
of the National Health Service, both past

00:08:40.029 --> 00:08:48.029
and present, and we will be doing that in
many venues across the country, not least

00:08:48.029 --> 00:08:55.269
at Westminster Abbey and York Minster in two
national services, but there are many other

00:08:55.269 --> 00:08:57.800
events going on around the country, too.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.070
Thank you, Simon.

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:03.180
Any questions or comments on the Chief Executive's
report?

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:04.180
No.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:10.329
Okay, thank you, and also, thank you very
much for your extraordinarily hard and successful

00:09:10.329 --> 00:09:13.339
work on discussions around the financial settlement.

00:09:13.339 --> 00:09:15.380
So, let's move on then, please.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:20.830
The next item on the agenda is developing
the NHS long-term plan, evidence-based interventions.

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:23.170
I'm going to invite Steve Powis to introduce
it.

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:24.170
Steve.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:25.170
Thank you, Malcolm.

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:29.269
So, I'm sure the Board will all agree that
where there is evidence of clinical and cost

00:09:29.269 --> 00:09:33.440
effectiveness, we should endeavour to introduce
new treatments into clinical practice as quickly

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:34.440
as possible.

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:38.690
However, it's just as important that we discontinue
treatments and interventions for which there

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:39.920
is evidence of ineffectiveness.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:45.350
There are a number of reasons for this, of
which I will highlight three.

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:49.650
First and foremost, we should not offer treatments
that at best disappoint or inconvenience our

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:52.500
patients, and at worst potentially harm them.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:57.759
Secondly, we should deploy our most precious
resource (our staff) to activities that are

00:09:57.759 --> 00:10:00.199
of proven clinical benefit.

00:10:00.199 --> 00:10:04.050
And third, of course, we should use taxpayers'
money wisely.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:07.779
The Board will recall it recently approved
guidance aimed at reducing the use of medicines

00:10:07.779 --> 00:10:12.250
within the NHS that have limited clinical
benefit, and we've now undertaken a similar

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:13.420
review of procedures.

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:18.740
To do this, we've worked with clinical experts
in a number of partner organisations; these

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:26.029
include NHS clinical commissioners, the Academy
of Medical Royal Colleges, NICE, and NHS Improvement,

00:10:26.029 --> 00:10:28.779
and we've also liaised with Healthwatch.

00:10:28.779 --> 00:10:32.600
Our approach has been to identify procedures
and interventions where there is evidence

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:35.079
of limited clinical benefit.

00:10:35.079 --> 00:10:39.029
We've derived that evidence base from a variety
of sources, including published clinical studies

00:10:39.029 --> 00:10:40.839
and NICE guidance.

00:10:40.839 --> 00:10:46.680
And using this methodology, we've generated
an initial list of 17 surgical procedures.

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:51.380
It's important to recognise that we are not
proposing a ban on these procedures; instead,

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:57.170
we are recommending that specific clinical
criteria are met before they are undertaken.

00:10:57.170 --> 00:11:01.290
We also recognise that many local commissioners
have already introduced similar guidelines.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:05.649
However, we know that there is marked variation
between CCGs with respect to the numbers of

00:11:05.649 --> 00:11:10.370
each procedure being undertaken, suggesting
that there has not been consistent development

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:13.670
or adoption of local guidelines.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:16.759
We plan to encourage implementation using
a number of techniques.

00:11:16.759 --> 00:11:20.440
These range from communication with commissioners
and providers, to monitoring of procedure

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:25.630
volumes and changes to tariff, and Ian will
talk a little bit more about that following

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:26.630
me.

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:31.930
So, we now wish to formally consult with the
public, patients and clinicians on our proposals,

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:35.110
and I therefore ask the Board to approve the
consultation document.

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:38.149
But first, I'm going to ask Ian to add a few
comments.

00:11:38.149 --> 00:11:39.580
Thank you very much, Steve.

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:45.990
We're very mindful that previous ambitions
to make progress in this area have not been

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:50.829
as successful as we would have liked, so we've
been working very closely with our partners

00:11:50.829 --> 00:11:56.410
to develop a suite of implementation actions
that mean the principles that Steve has highlighted

00:11:56.410 --> 00:12:06.360
will now be effected, and that includes working
through and setting activity reduction goals,

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:13.550
which we're consulting on formally; ensuring
that there is a guiding coalition of national

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:21.360
interests to progress this work; effective
multi-channel clinical engagement, working

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:28.040
with a number of exemplar geographies, who
will be able to show how they can implement

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:35.439
all of these changes at the same time faster
than the rest of the country; changes to the

00:12:35.439 --> 00:12:44.600
tariff system, backed by a proposed adjustment
to the NHS standard contract; changes to the

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:52.689
e-referral system, so that we better reflect
this as GPs refer into secondary care; constructing

00:12:52.689 --> 00:13:00.600
a joined-up monitoring dashboard; changes
to audit arrangements; consideration of how

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:06.050
we reflect this in our assurance systems;
as well as the support of the Care Quality

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:10.709
Commission in aligning their activity with
the programme.

00:13:10.709 --> 00:13:11.790
Thank you, Steve.

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:13.959
Well, thank you both very much.

00:13:13.959 --> 00:13:18.410
Can we just perhaps open some of the detail
of this?

00:13:18.410 --> 00:13:24.140
I understand it's very difficult to estimate
what might be the cost savings that might

00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:32.029
arise from this, but - and one wonders, is
this the first, do you think, of several future

00:13:32.029 --> 00:13:37.339
interventions at national level, which are
intended not only to save money and cut out

00:13:37.339 --> 00:13:42.130
unwarranted variation, but also to improve
the quality of care to patients?

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:45.360
I'm trying to get a sense of how the balance
of this is.

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:52.709
It's about wasteful procedures which are potentially
harmful, but to what extent does this paper

00:13:52.709 --> 00:13:58.800
actually capture the vast array of unwarranted
variation that we have across the NHS?

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:04.269
So, we started with a longer list, which we
have narrowed down to a list of 17, which

00:14:04.269 --> 00:14:09.160
we believe are the right list to address initially.

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:17.050
There are reasons why some of the other procedures
or interventions we looked at are not on this

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:21.800
list; for instance, around the mechanisms
that we might be able to use to reduce variation,

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:24.930
which we would need to do further work on,
where they are not directly commissioned but

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:29.350
are part of a pathway of care rather than
an individual commissioned item.

00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:32.490
So, I think this will be the first stage.

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.920
The same principles will apply that I've outlined.

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:40.829
And indeed, as part of the consultation, one
of the consultation questions is to ask whether

00:14:40.829 --> 00:14:46.610
there are procedures that people feel: whether
they're clinicians or public or patients - that

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:49.520
we should consider in a further round.

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:53.269
Let me invite questions and comments from
the Board.

00:14:53.269 --> 00:14:54.269
Michelle?

00:14:54.269 --> 00:14:55.999
Thanks, Steve.

00:14:55.999 --> 00:15:05.069
Could you talk us through the data analysis
work you've done to understand the impact

00:15:05.069 --> 00:15:09.550
of these proposals on different communities
and equality groups?

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:14.029
Yes, we've done an equality assessment, and
we don't believe it will have a significant

00:15:14.029 --> 00:15:19.640
impact, but I think that's a very important
point, and I think we will continue to look

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:22.250
at that closely in the consultation period.

00:15:22.250 --> 00:15:23.389
Thank you.

00:15:23.389 --> 00:15:26.060
Can I just pick up on one point?

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:29.009
Sorry to monopolise it, but it's the Chair's
privilege, after all.

00:15:29.009 --> 00:15:32.930
But one point, which is, you know, there has
been media comment that this is about cutting

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:37.759
expenditure, but my understanding, of course,
is this is actually recycling expenditure

00:15:37.759 --> 00:15:41.949
from activity which we think is wasteful or
even potentially harmful to patients, and

00:15:41.949 --> 00:15:44.779
putting it back into the provision of other
services.

00:15:44.779 --> 00:15:45.779
Yes, absolutely.

00:15:45.779 --> 00:15:51.509
So, my opening comment was for a reason, which
is, if we do want to invest in clinically

00:15:51.509 --> 00:15:59.079
proven treatments - and I think the good news
is that there are lots of very interesting

00:15:59.079 --> 00:16:04.529
new techniques and procedures on the horizon
- then where there is an evidence base that

00:16:04.529 --> 00:16:08.029
those are clinically effective, and when they
are cost effective, we should absolutely be

00:16:08.029 --> 00:16:12.139
attempting to get those into clinical practice
as quickly as possible.

00:16:12.139 --> 00:16:19.639
And of course, in recycling the funding that
we are currently expending on interventions

00:16:19.639 --> 00:16:24.309
or procedures that are now no longer thought
to be clinically effective or have been superseded

00:16:24.309 --> 00:16:29.279
by different procedures, then absolutely,
that resource should be recycled through to

00:16:29.279 --> 00:16:33.399
fund the clinically effective, evidence-based
procedures that we wish to introduce in the

00:16:33.399 --> 00:16:34.439
future.

00:16:34.439 --> 00:16:37.060
Yes, Richard, and then David.

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:45.810
I think one of the comments you made about,
This is not about a ban on these procedures;

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:52.800
what reassurance can you give people that
this won't effectively develop into a ban?

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:59.620
So, the sort of mechanisms for restricting
of funding, except in particular cases: can

00:16:59.620 --> 00:17:05.650
people be reassured that that will still allow
proper clinical decision-making on circumstances

00:17:05.650 --> 00:17:07.970
where this - these would be appropriate?

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:09.930
So, I make two comments.

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:13.430
Firstly, clinicians are very used to guidelines.

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:18.839
We actually are an exemplar in terms of a
country in developing guidelines; I give you

00:17:18.839 --> 00:17:20.970
NICE as an example.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:29.280
So, the concept of guidelines, consensus opinion,
which clinicians refer to is well established.

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:35.620
Secondly, CCGs and local commissioners have
established processes for reviewing individual

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:41.880
cases, and we anticipate that those will be
used as they are at present in local guidelines,

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:43.890
and they will be used to effect these processes.

00:17:43.890 --> 00:17:50.770
Could I add briefly, one of the reasons why
we haven't made as much progress in the past

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:56.910
is because there's been a gap between the
evidence and the high-level guidance produced,

00:17:56.910 --> 00:17:58.640
and then what clinicians can actually do.

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:03.550
So, as part of this process, Steve and his
team have developed clinical criteria for

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:09.080
all of the 17 interventions working with the
professions, which set out the circumstances

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:14.650
in which the interventions should and shouldn't
normally be applied, quite apart from the

00:18:14.650 --> 00:18:20.250
question of the individual exceptional cases,
and we're consulting on all of those criteria

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:21.250
through this process.

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:26.490
And the reason it isn't a ban is because we
perfectly accept that no guideline perfectly

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:31.640
fits every single patient, and there will
always be exceptions where it is quite right

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:36.201
and there is a limited evidence base that
for a particular individual with a particular

00:18:36.201 --> 00:18:40.360
set of clinical circumstances, it would be
quite appropriate to proceed with that.

00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:44.220
So, that is recognised, that is the skill
of the individual clinician in consultation,

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:46.410
and a shared decision with the patient.

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:51.620
And as I said, there will be mechanisms in
place in order for those procedures to be

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:52.620
undertaken.

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:53.620
Thank you.

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:54.620
David?

00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:59.310
So, Steve, first of all, I think this is a
- should be an ongoing process.

00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:02.870
I know it's one of the questions you ask in
the consultation, but it seems to be inevitable

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:09.260
that as medical science advances, things will
go up and need to be replaced, so I'd personally

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:10.570
be very supportive.

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:17.640
I think the - my most sort of question, I
guess, is in virtually all of these instances,

00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:25.380
we're - and I'm paraphrasing and reducing
to make the point - is that we're stopping

00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:32.820
a surgical procedure and replacing it with
some form of ongoing support or help, which

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:40.090
will require different skills, different money
flows and, to some extent, changes in behaviour

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:41.842
in both clinicians but also in patients.

00:19:41.842 --> 00:19:49.960
And I wonder whether we've given thought as
part of this to how we're going to be confident

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:56.810
that those things will happen, and that the
saving over here won't just be taken to allow

00:19:56.810 --> 00:20:02.770
more surgical prevention, and then there won't
be sufficient resources in people capability,

00:20:02.770 --> 00:20:08.110
cash, whatever, to provide what essentially
we're doing, which is replacing with a better,

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:09.870
more clinically proven approach.

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:12.320
Is that a fair summary, by the way?

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:13.320
It's a fair summary.

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:17.810
So, I think most of the alternative procedures
are already in play, so we are not inventing

00:20:17.810 --> 00:20:18.810
new approaches.

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:19.810
Yeah, understand that.

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:23.460
We have netted off in our assumptions, and
I think we've been cautious in our financial

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:28.550
analysis, because we recognise that we are
not replacing something with nothing; we're

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:31.860
replacing something with something else, which
will have another cost profile.

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:39.310
And that is why the cost analysis inevitably
is a judgement, and we will need to track,

00:20:39.310 --> 00:20:41.210
but I think those are fair comments.

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:45.490
The thing is, for me, the cost approach is
secondary to the medical effectiveness.

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:46.620
Absolutely, yes.

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:51.420
And the opportunity cost of talented people.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:59.220
But I guess as we think about the implementation
of this, it does - you know, we talk a lot

00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:03.210
about guidelines and those things, which have
some effectiveness, different in different

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:04.210
places.

00:21:04.210 --> 00:21:10.130
I'd encourage us to give as much thought as
to how we ensure that CCGs and practitioners

00:21:10.130 --> 00:21:16.510
make sure that the capacity and the capabilities
are in place at the right sort of scale to

00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:22.500
support people for whom we're not offering
route A, we're now saying route B.

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:23.770
Yeah, I agree.

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:28.070
Okay, well, thank you both Steve and Ian for
the work that's gone into this.

00:21:28.070 --> 00:21:33.020
I'm going to ask the Board first of all to
approve the launch of the public consultation

00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:35.750
in terms of the document that we've got in
front of us.

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:36.750
Are - we're happy to do that?

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:42.170
And you might like to say a few words about
the processes of engagement and how we can

00:21:42.170 --> 00:21:48.010
be sure that we're reaching out to the - not
just the clinicians, but to patients and to

00:21:48.010 --> 00:21:49.040
the public more generally.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:55.030
Yeah, so we have a section on page 29, I think,
of the guidance - at least, the version I've

00:21:55.030 --> 00:22:01.570
got in front of me - which outlines the process
for responding to the consultation, so that

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:04.640
includes an online web form, written responses.

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:08.360
We will be holding events, face-to-face events,
and webinars.

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:12.840
And of course, we will be reviewing that during
the consultation process, and if we need to

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:16.240
put on additional events or additional processes,
we will do that, too.

00:22:16.240 --> 00:22:21.480
Okay, well, thank you, and we look forward
to hearing the outcomes of the consultation

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:23.570
in due course and coming to formal decisions.

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:26.580
So, today is an approval of the consultation.

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:27.580
Yes, correct.

00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:30.030
Formal decisions await the next iteration.

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:32.570
Okay, thank you very much for that.

00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:39.150
May we then move on to the next item, Ian,
which is the primary care reform papers.

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:40.740
Please, over to you.

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:42.250
Thank you very much, Chair.

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:49.370
So, the time is ripe for the most significant
discussions about the GP contract since 2004.

00:22:49.370 --> 00:22:51.640
This is for five reasons.

00:22:51.640 --> 00:22:58.650
First, the long-term NHS plan, which will
build on the GP Forward View and go further.

00:22:58.650 --> 00:23:03.140
Second, the ongoing pressures on general practice,
including recruitment, retention and skill

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:07.240
mix, which Dominic will touch on in the later
paper.

00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:12.420
Third, the nationwide development of primary
care networks as an expanding service delivery

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:13.420
unit.

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:17.870
Fourth, the potential of digital primary care
to benefit patients and practices alike.

00:23:17.870 --> 00:23:24.800
And fifth, the array of different major reviews
now underway: the Quality and Outcomes Framework,

00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:30.220
the Carr-Hill funding formula, the review
of GP premises, the review of the GP partnership

00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:35.850
model, as well as the government's commitment
to introduce a state-backed indemnity solution.

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:42.480
And across all of these different elements,
we need to create a coherent approach, and

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:49.470
that calls for closer partnership working
with the profession to develop and agree what

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:55.660
could be wide-ranging contract reforms to
enable general practice to flourish into the

00:23:55.660 --> 00:23:56.660
next decade.

00:23:56.660 --> 00:24:03.780
NHS England has now in-housed the negotiations
from NHS employers, and I look forward to

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:09.980
working with Richard Vautrey and his GPC England
team to co-design and reach agreement about

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:15.060
the main building blocks of reform, as well
as making progress for 2019/20.

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:24.930
Today, NHS England is seeking to launch engagement
exercises on two specific facets of the GP

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:31.210
contract: the Quality and Outcomes Framework,
and how we pay for Digital First models.

00:24:31.210 --> 00:24:38.490
The Commissioning Committee considered both
of these in May earlier this year.

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:44.940
In 2017, NHS England committed to reviewing
the Quality and Outcomes Framework.

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:50.840
We established a dedicated team, a structured
review methodology, including a thorough review

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:54.540
of the evidence base, and a broad-based steering
group.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:25:01.420
We engaged widely, and today we're publishing
the outcome of the review, and I'd like to

00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:07.980
thank members of the steering group for their
invaluable contributions and advice.

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:13.790
It's worth noting that QOF is both an incentive
scheme, but it also provides core practice

00:25:13.790 --> 00:25:15.130
income.

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:19.470
The profession has called for it at different
times to be scrapped, and also for it to be

00:25:19.470 --> 00:25:20.590
retained.

00:25:20.590 --> 00:25:25.530
It's led to positive outcomes, but it can
also get in the way of GPs providing the most

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:27.100
appropriate holistic care.

00:25:27.100 --> 00:25:33.660
And the review has sought to balance the risks
of over-retirement of indicators with indicator

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:38.010
reform, and the creation of headroom for innovation.

00:25:38.010 --> 00:25:44.420
And through careful deliberation, the review
makes five main recommendations: to modify

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:52.390
up to half of the indicators to improve efficacy
and impact; to update the exception reporting

00:25:52.390 --> 00:25:59.840
mechanisms, to enable smarter differentiation
between different groups of patients; thirdly,

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:05.740
to retire up to a quarter of indicators through
a transparent indicator assessment methodology;

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:12.970
fourth, to introduce a new quality improvement
domain, which would utilise points freed up

00:26:12.970 --> 00:26:21.280
through retiring indicators; and fifth, to
run a national trial of a network-level QOF

00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:23.310
with a select number of sites.

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:31.210
NHS England has also reviewed payments for
digital-first primary care.

00:26:31.210 --> 00:26:37.130
Over the next decade, we will be supporting
faster, full adoption of digital primary care,

00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:44.760
and the way in which we contract and pay for
care needs to keep up with digital delivery.

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:50.600
And those payment mechanisms need to be fair,
and be seen to be fair.

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:56.890
We conclude from the evidence currently available
that there are three specific ways that payments

00:26:56.890 --> 00:27:05.470
need to change to avoid an unjustifiable redistribution
of practice income to digital-first models,

00:27:05.470 --> 00:27:10.930
and these all relate to the geographical relationship
between the practice and its patients.

00:27:10.930 --> 00:27:16.590
So, first, the rurality index, we think, should
apply only to patients who live in a practice's

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:19.880
catchment area, not all registered patients.

00:27:19.880 --> 00:27:25.860
Secondly, the 2.18-per-patient London adjustment
should only apply to patients who live in

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:31.140
London, not to patients living outside London
who are registered with a London practice.

00:27:31.140 --> 00:27:36.760
And third, that the payments for out-of-area
patients should not include the costs of home

00:27:36.760 --> 00:27:40.120
visits, which the practice doesn't have to
provide.

00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:44.480
The analysis and the proposals in our paper
are a first step.

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:48.980
We freely acknowledge that we don't have the
full answer here.

00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:56.140
We will need to learn and further adapt as
we support further, faster expansion of digital-first

00:27:56.140 --> 00:28:04.020
models, and understand better their cost structure,
patient characteristics and their impact on

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:07.350
wider system utilisation.

00:28:07.350 --> 00:28:13.500
We would welcome views on both documents by
31st August to inform the formal discussions

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:20.710
we will then be having with the BMA about
potential contract reform from 2019/20.

00:28:20.710 --> 00:28:26.010
And I ask the Board, I think, to do three
things: first, to note that we're now entering

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:33.480
this new phase of contract reform; secondly,
to approve both of the attached reports; and

00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:38.160
with that, to launch the engagement exercises
on the QOF review and payments for digital-first

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:39.260
primary care.

00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:40.960
Well, thank you very much for that, Ian.

00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:45.160
I mean, this is, as you said in your very
opening comments, the most radical - potentially

00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:49.280
the most radical review of primary care and
its funding since 2004.

00:28:49.280 --> 00:28:52.220
Can I just ask a technical question?

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:57.380
You're - you talk in the paper about engagement
as opposed to consultation; what are the differences

00:28:57.380 --> 00:28:59.860
that you foresee in that process?

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:07.090
So, we've already engaged fully with the profession
and patient groups through the QOF review,

00:29:07.090 --> 00:29:10.980
and there's been a large number of different
workshops and events.

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:15.220
We've similarly had a lot of conversations
around the Digital First process.

00:29:15.220 --> 00:29:22.030
We're now formally inviting views and feedback
on these documents.

00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:29.750
Before we then go into the process of contract
reform discussions, those would then potentially

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:34.590
take effect through regulations, which the
Department of Health has responsibility for

00:29:34.590 --> 00:29:36.470
implementing.

00:29:36.470 --> 00:29:41.180
But we are in effect their agent in those
negotiations.

00:29:41.180 --> 00:29:46.980
We have, I think, the formal responsibility
in law to lead on the development of the contract,

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:53.640
and we have the financial responsibility and
the function, but they've retained the legal

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:56.870
obligation in relation to the specific regulations.

00:29:56.870 --> 00:29:57.870
Good.

00:29:57.870 --> 00:29:59.510
Okay, thank you.

00:29:59.510 --> 00:30:04.180
So, let me invite some questions and comments,
and I think we can look at the overall and

00:30:04.180 --> 00:30:06.100
then at the QOF, and then at the Digital First.

00:30:06.100 --> 00:30:08.870
But let me start with Noel and Wendy.

00:30:08.870 --> 00:30:14.310
My comment, Malcolm, was really on Digital
First.

00:30:14.310 --> 00:30:15.440
Do you want to take that first?

00:30:15.440 --> 00:30:17.830
Shall we go to Digital First first, then?

00:30:17.830 --> 00:30:18.830
Yes.

00:30:18.830 --> 00:30:20.740
Mine was on Digital First as well, actually.

00:30:20.740 --> 00:30:23.450
Okay, let's do Digital First, not second.

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:24.450
Okay, Noel?

00:30:24.450 --> 00:30:29.880
So, we sort of recognise societally that people
live complex lives, and they don't always

00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:37.400
want access to GP services in their home,
but they also want it in the city, too.

00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:43.680
What is the implication for the three tariff
reform measures, Ian, on the marketplace that

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:50.510
we want to stimulate for digital services
that accommodate complex lives of people who

00:30:50.510 --> 00:30:55.630
work in cities but don't necessarily live
in cities?

00:30:55.630 --> 00:31:02.510
The proposals here are intended to support
the sustainable faster development of those

00:31:02.510 --> 00:31:11.310
models, and they're intended to do that by
ensuring that the way in which we pay for

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:18.590
patients who are - don't have such a close
geographical relationship with their patients

00:31:18.590 --> 00:31:27.680
are fair and sustainable, and appear as such,
I think, to the outside world.

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:31.230
Noel, are you reflecting?

00:31:31.230 --> 00:31:40.040
Well, I was reflecting on the fact that GP
choice was a design to enable people to have

00:31:40.040 --> 00:31:47.520
that mobility of service access, and I just
think it's important when we design these

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:54.470
policies that we consider how it affects some
of the fundamental principles of what we are

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:59.340
providing in terms of service access, but
also on the way it's stimulating a marketplace

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:03.730
to come forward to provide alternatives to
physical channels.

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:06.680
So, that was my point.

00:32:06.680 --> 00:32:16.610
And these relatively technical specific adjustments
do not in any way impact adversely on GP choice

00:32:16.610 --> 00:32:19.770
policy, which remains exactly as it is.

00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:21.530
Chair, can I?

00:32:21.530 --> 00:32:23.310
Yes, Matthew, please.

00:32:23.310 --> 00:32:24.400
Thank you.

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:27.230
Because this has been a really - the work
that's gone behind this has been a really

00:32:27.230 --> 00:32:34.120
good example of Ian's strategy team working
together with my digital team to try and work

00:32:34.120 --> 00:32:39.500
out what the strategy has to look like in
the future with this digital dimension coming

00:32:39.500 --> 00:32:40.500
across.

00:32:40.500 --> 00:32:47.200
And I think there is a significant element
to this, which is to say, as we move forward

00:32:47.200 --> 00:32:52.530
and we learn, we need to make sure that the
funding flows reflect the changes in the models

00:32:52.530 --> 00:33:00.420
of access, both in terms of paying digital
providers a fair rate, which means not - a

00:33:00.420 --> 00:33:03.840
rate that they can actually operate at, but
not also an excess rate, because what we don't

00:33:03.840 --> 00:33:08.840
want to do is to find that market entrants
are only successful for a couple of years,

00:33:08.840 --> 00:33:12.080
and then when we change the pricing, they
exit it, so it needs - we need to get it fair

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:13.450
as quickly as possible.

00:33:13.450 --> 00:33:18.070
But also, to reflect that it's changing the
case mix, maybe changing the case mix, for

00:33:18.070 --> 00:33:22.550
patients who want a face-to-face contact with
a GP, and that GPs are appropriately paid

00:33:22.550 --> 00:33:23.750
for those as well.

00:33:23.750 --> 00:33:28.240
So, this is, as you say, potentially a very
big change, going forward, over the next five,

00:33:28.240 --> 00:33:31.780
ten years in the way in which primary care
and urgent access is provided.

00:33:31.780 --> 00:33:36.860
We'll include digital in it, and we need to
be quite agile to make sure that it works

00:33:36.860 --> 00:33:41.630
for people who want to innovate, but also
supports the vast number of people who use

00:33:41.630 --> 00:33:48.010
primary care who are elderly, have long-term
conditions, and enjoy and find important the

00:33:48.010 --> 00:33:51.260
personal relationship with the GP that they've
got to know over many years.

00:33:51.260 --> 00:33:53.690
We need to be able to support both services
simultaneously.

00:33:53.690 --> 00:33:55.370
That's helpful, thank you.

00:33:55.370 --> 00:33:56.370
Wendy?

00:33:56.370 --> 00:34:00.180
Yeah, I mean, this leads in a bit to my comment.

00:34:00.180 --> 00:34:06.011
We obviously spent quite a long time on this,
Ian, on both the engagements at the Commissioning

00:34:06.011 --> 00:34:09.679
Committee, and I think the Committee felt
comfortable with going forward to get further

00:34:09.679 --> 00:34:12.340
input, which we would seek.

00:34:12.340 --> 00:34:19.690
My question on the Digital First piece is
really about - I think our learning in that

00:34:19.690 --> 00:34:24.179
experience is there's a lot going on, there's
a lot more that's going to go on, and we can

00:34:24.179 --> 00:34:28.870
only have so much sense of how we'll be able
to anticipate the future.

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:34.350
How do we create, in these negotiations, an
environment that continues to encourage innovation,

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:40.060
as we said, but also gives us the space to
not have to re-look at these funding models

00:34:40.060 --> 00:34:41.820
and things every six months?

00:34:41.820 --> 00:34:44.690
How do we develop that with enough flexibility?

00:34:44.690 --> 00:34:50.860
I'd say that the most important thing is that
we're really clear, as we develop the primary

00:34:50.860 --> 00:34:56.960
care elements of the long-term plan, where
we want to try and get to; where investment

00:34:56.960 --> 00:35:02.730
is coming, what our ambitions are around change;
and then how we line up what the necessary

00:35:02.730 --> 00:35:07.140
actions are to support that, and then in the
light of that, think about what the contractual

00:35:07.140 --> 00:35:09.590
arrangements ought to be.

00:35:09.590 --> 00:35:17.850
I think we've got an opportunity to have a
smarter way of working directly with the BMA

00:35:17.850 --> 00:35:26.470
as we develop the plan, to understand then
what the medium-term contractual changes are,

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:32.060
rather than be trapped in what has recently
been an annual negotiation cycle where we're

00:35:32.060 --> 00:35:34.980
looking at relatively modest incremental steps.

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:41.980
And part of the opportunity arising from this
reset, really, I think, of the relationships

00:35:41.980 --> 00:35:47.980
is that we can look at the medium-term changes
that are needed, and then think about 19/20

00:35:47.980 --> 00:35:53.250
within that context, rather than the other
way round.

00:35:53.250 --> 00:35:54.250
Other - David?

00:35:54.250 --> 00:36:04.260
Again, on digital - I think the other one
I think we'll come to - but Digital First

00:36:04.260 --> 00:36:06.520
doesn't mean digital only, is that right?

00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:11.320
Did I read that right?

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:12.320
Yes.

00:36:12.320 --> 00:36:13.320
Okay.

00:36:13.320 --> 00:36:23.680
And how do we - so, to what extent does the
- because I suspect we will see hybrid models

00:36:23.680 --> 00:36:26.110
start to emerge.

00:36:26.110 --> 00:36:32.340
To what extent does this set of consultation
work for those types of models rather than

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:34.580
digital only?

00:36:34.580 --> 00:36:44.200
So, these three relatively narrow, specific
changes are changes to the entirety of how

00:36:44.200 --> 00:36:51.970
we pay for general practice services, including
physical only, hybrid models and digital first

00:36:51.970 --> 00:36:52.970
only.

00:36:52.970 --> 00:36:57.420
And as we've developed them, we have considered
the impact, and we've set out some of the

00:36:57.420 --> 00:37:03.110
impacts on physical only and the hybrid.

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:09.880
I think there is a - there's a - one of the
most important parts of the ten-year vision

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:18.790
and the five-year delivery plan will be our
stance on how we think about the development

00:37:18.790 --> 00:37:20.080
of different models.

00:37:20.080 --> 00:37:25.260
Because every other industry tells you, going
through digital disruption, that actually,

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:31.860
the evolutional pace picks up, and I think
- to the point that was made before, Wendy's

00:37:31.860 --> 00:37:38.500
point, we need to be also engaging on the
future direction and the implications of that,

00:37:38.500 --> 00:37:45.590
as well as on getting greater equality and
equity in today's world.

00:37:45.590 --> 00:37:47.980
Exactly so.

00:37:47.980 --> 00:37:48.980
Matthew and Joanne.

00:37:48.980 --> 00:37:53.380
Can I just - it's really just to clarify with
Ian that the Carr-Hill model, the funding

00:37:53.380 --> 00:37:56.620
formula, doesn't allow a digital-only model.

00:37:56.620 --> 00:38:02.000
You have to be able to access and see a GP
through that triage, through that model.

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:06.640
So, behind these models that this funding
formula's talking about, somewhere there is

00:38:06.640 --> 00:38:10.400
a GP who will see you if you need to see - if
that is the right point to get to.

00:38:10.400 --> 00:38:13.100
It's a - that's why it's Digital First.

00:38:13.100 --> 00:38:14.100
Thank you.

00:38:14.100 --> 00:38:17.650
I mean, this is a slightly odd conversation,
isn't it?

00:38:17.650 --> 00:38:24.000
Because we've backed into a really big, important
development subject, which is multi-channel

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:30.060
primary care, through a paper with a very
specific purpose, which is to smooth out the

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:32.820
unintended consequences of a particular pilot.

00:38:32.820 --> 00:38:38.350
And I think we just need to keep that in mind,
and really, the bigger conversation is being

00:38:38.350 --> 00:38:42.450
held within the organisation in different
ways, and we haven't ventilated it publicly

00:38:42.450 --> 00:38:44.850
quite yet, is my understanding.

00:38:44.850 --> 00:38:50.650
The only observation I'd make is the reason
that we've ended up where we are, which is

00:38:50.650 --> 00:38:55.620
in the public Board looking at the consultation
on this narrow set of changes to smooth the

00:38:55.620 --> 00:39:01.120
effects of the pilot, is because of the dramatic
effect this pilot has had.

00:39:01.120 --> 00:39:08.170
And I think that, you know, it's as well to
see that as an indicator of public appetite

00:39:08.170 --> 00:39:15.180
for this, and how important this broader topic
is of how to introduce multi-channel, including

00:39:15.180 --> 00:39:19.310
digital primary care, and what that might
look like in the future.

00:39:19.310 --> 00:39:21.670
I think that's exactly right, Joanne.

00:39:21.670 --> 00:39:26.670
I mean, just one additional point I'd make
is that we haven't designed these changes

00:39:26.670 --> 00:39:30.400
solely in relation to the GP at hand service.

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:36.830
Obviously, that's the most well-known, and
has been growing fastest in terms of new registrations,

00:39:36.830 --> 00:39:42.460
but we've carefully sought to develop this
for all potential equivalent arrangements.

00:39:42.460 --> 00:39:44.370
I'll take that,.

00:39:44.370 --> 00:39:47.560
Thank you Ian, that's helpful.

00:39:47.560 --> 00:39:48.560
Victor?

00:39:48.560 --> 00:39:50.750
Just a question really.

00:39:50.750 --> 00:39:58.520
I know you haven't - these suggestions around
the payment system aren't led by this particular

00:39:58.520 --> 00:40:00.840
pilot, because we're still evaluating it,
I understand.

00:40:00.840 --> 00:40:01.840
Is that right?

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:03.900
We're still evaluating the GP at hand plan.

00:40:03.900 --> 00:40:05.490
At least, that's what the indications are.

00:40:05.490 --> 00:40:06.490
Yes.

00:40:06.490 --> 00:40:09.000
So, the question I was going to ask is: is
this the only pilot?

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:12.490
Are there other pilots that we're also seeking
to learn from?

00:40:12.490 --> 00:40:22.500
And does this - do your recommendations leave
room for adjustments in what we plan to do

00:40:22.500 --> 00:40:27.090
as a result of the outcome of these pilots,
including the one in Hammersmith and Fulham?

00:40:27.090 --> 00:40:33.600
As one - as in, one sparrow does not a summer
make, I think is the analogy.

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:43.440
So, we are conducting - or strictly accurately,
the CCG is conducting an evaluation through

00:40:43.440 --> 00:40:49.060
the London region of the Hammersmith GP at
hand model, because it is a significant shift,

00:40:49.060 --> 00:40:56.060
and therefore - which is being implemented
not as a national pilot, but as a primary

00:40:56.060 --> 00:41:02.330
care offering developed by the local GPs to
the public as a change that is going on in

00:41:02.330 --> 00:41:03.330
the system.

00:41:03.330 --> 00:41:07.130
We are evaluating that as we would any other
major and disruptive change that happened

00:41:07.130 --> 00:41:09.040
anywhere, we would want to understand that.

00:41:09.040 --> 00:41:13.280
The piece of work that Ian's team has done
has been to move quickly to say, it is clear

00:41:13.280 --> 00:41:19.020
that you can see early on that this is changing
some of the underlying dynamics under which

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:25.250
the formula is based, and therefore, what
is it clear we should act on now?

00:41:25.250 --> 00:41:29.980
I think we will need to iterate this over
time as we learn more, as different models

00:41:29.980 --> 00:41:30.980
emerge.

00:41:30.980 --> 00:41:33.050
Because GP at hand won't be the only model.

00:41:33.050 --> 00:41:40.420
We also have - we've got 300 practices and
three million people who have e-front ends

00:41:40.420 --> 00:41:46.780
to their GP practice, which are deeply embedded,
which are an alternate route as opposed to

00:41:46.780 --> 00:41:51.830
a digital-first route, but equally, over time
could become significant in changing the way

00:41:51.830 --> 00:41:53.140
in which practices run.

00:41:53.140 --> 00:41:58.060
So, this won't be the only time we come back
to the funding model conversation.

00:41:58.060 --> 00:42:05.150
And we haven't even for the - for this consultation,
we acknowledge that we haven't looked at the

00:42:05.150 --> 00:42:09.220
question of the patient characteristics.

00:42:09.220 --> 00:42:16.220
This is only looking at the relationship of
the geography of the practice and the individual

00:42:16.220 --> 00:42:20.950
patients, and we know there's more work to
be done to understand that in relation to

00:42:20.950 --> 00:42:27.820
all Digital First models, as well as the cost
structure of Digital First models, and their

00:42:27.820 --> 00:42:31.330
effects on wider system utilisation, so A&amp;E
attendances, prescribing rates, admissions

00:42:31.330 --> 00:42:32.330
and so on.

00:42:32.330 --> 00:42:38.410
While Ive got you, just a very quick question:
could you say a bit more about what you've

00:42:38.410 --> 00:42:44.390
learnt about equality and equity in relation
to Digital First?

00:42:44.390 --> 00:42:54.470
So, we've undertaken an equalities impact
assessment, and we don't think that there

00:42:54.470 --> 00:43:00.640
would be adverse consequences as a result
of these specific sets of changes, Victor.

00:43:00.640 --> 00:43:09.380
I think the bigger question of equalities
I think will inevitably be picked up more

00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:14.460
fully within the consideration of the patient
characteristics within Digital First models,

00:43:14.460 --> 00:43:18.980
rather than these specific, narrower changes.

00:43:18.980 --> 00:43:22.520
Okay, well, thank you very much for that.

00:43:22.520 --> 00:43:28.200
I think around the Board, we understand that
primary care, and particularly GP services,

00:43:28.200 --> 00:43:33.560
are the fundamental building block of a universal
healthcare system in this country.

00:43:33.560 --> 00:43:37.410
Secondly, we also understand that it's under
great pressure and under great stress, and

00:43:37.410 --> 00:43:43.650
we've spent a lot of time over the past two
years trying to redirect investment into GP

00:43:43.650 --> 00:43:49.930
services, and this seems to me to be a logical
and vital continuation of that.

00:43:49.930 --> 00:43:55.710
We also, of course, understand that there
will be models of Digital First coming through,

00:43:55.710 --> 00:44:00.400
many of them initiated within practices; Matthew,
the model that you were talking about, the

00:44:00.400 --> 00:44:01.740
e-front end.

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:07.330
But also, there will be a very vibrant private
sector looking to universalise digital models

00:44:07.330 --> 00:44:13.340
of primary care, and I would suggest that
behind these rather technical amendments,

00:44:13.340 --> 00:44:20.170
as you characterise them, lies the more fundamental
question of how we combine those two in order

00:44:20.170 --> 00:44:27.230
to ensure that Digital First becomes a model
that supports GPs, that reduces workload,

00:44:27.230 --> 00:44:34.580
that allows greater efficiency and a greater
ability for GPs to concentrate on those patients

00:44:34.580 --> 00:44:36.200
to whom they can provide greater spend.

00:44:36.200 --> 00:44:43.500
So, it's part of our looking at GP networks,
it's part of our reinvestment in primary care,

00:44:43.500 --> 00:44:47.490
and - but also understanding, as I think Wendy
put it, that actually, change is going to

00:44:47.490 --> 00:44:52.520
happen very quickly across this area, and
we've got a fundamental duty, I think, to

00:44:52.520 --> 00:44:57.570
ensure that there is continuity of care for
our patients that's made increasingly efficient

00:44:57.570 --> 00:45:02.850
and effective and accurate by virtue of the
deployment of new technologies.

00:45:02.850 --> 00:45:06.641
So, I hope that puts the context in which
we will go out to further engagement - but

00:45:06.641 --> 00:45:12.050
can I just, on Digital First, commend the
work that you're doing with the BMA and with

00:45:12.050 --> 00:45:13.050
other stakeholders?

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:19.530
I think this is an appropriate, cautious way
of picking up some of the perhaps unanticipated

00:45:19.530 --> 00:45:26.110
consequences of the GP at hand issue, whilst
at the same time realising that the funding

00:45:26.110 --> 00:45:29.900
formula more generally is in need of review.

00:45:29.900 --> 00:45:33.860
So, may we then move to the QOF paper?

00:45:33.860 --> 00:45:38.420
And let me invite - I'm sorry, just before
I do, Steve, did you want to come in on this?

00:45:38.420 --> 00:45:40.160
I was going to talk about QOF.

00:45:40.160 --> 00:45:41.160
Okay.

00:45:41.160 --> 00:45:42.920
Right, well may we move to the QOF paper?

00:45:42.920 --> 00:45:43.920
Steve.

00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:48.380
So, on QOF, I was just going to say how pleased
I was that there will be a domain for quality

00:45:48.380 --> 00:45:49.830
improvement in QOF.

00:45:49.830 --> 00:45:56.830
So, I personally believe that quality improvement
is something that increasingly is important

00:45:56.830 --> 00:46:01.700
in our improvement of healthcare, and strikes
a real chord amongst practising clinicians

00:46:01.700 --> 00:46:07.900
and really gets them into a place where they
come together with colleagues, multi-disciplinary

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:10.750
colleagues, and manages really to focus on
improvement.

00:46:10.750 --> 00:46:16.380
So, my question was: how confident are we
that we will be able to support practices

00:46:16.380 --> 00:46:19.120
in undertaking quality improvement work?

00:46:19.120 --> 00:46:23.190
I think it's a great question, Steve.

00:46:23.190 --> 00:46:29.140
I think we're still at the - notwithstanding
all the work over the last year, we're still

00:46:29.140 --> 00:46:31.460
nearer the beginning of the process here.

00:46:31.460 --> 00:46:38.230
We've got the engagement exercise, we will
then have the detailed work of discussing

00:46:38.230 --> 00:46:44.740
and agreeing the precise changes to the indicators,
including - and obviously, we'll be looking

00:46:44.740 --> 00:46:48.521
to reach agreement with the profession that
there is a quality improvement domain, and

00:46:48.521 --> 00:46:52.080
that we've got the three elements that we're
describing here.

00:46:52.080 --> 00:46:56.370
As part of those conversations, we'll need
to work through the practicalities of the

00:46:56.370 --> 00:47:04.300
implementation, and I think we're all mindful
in NHS England, with our national partners

00:47:04.300 --> 00:47:12.430
and in the profession, that we have been under-clubbed
in terms of support for quality improvement,

00:47:12.430 --> 00:47:17.680
and therefore, there will be a need to consider
that very carefully as we think through the

00:47:17.680 --> 00:47:19.580
way in which we give effect to any changes.

00:47:19.580 --> 00:47:22.369
Good, well thank you very much.

00:47:22.369 --> 00:47:26.140
Are there any other issues arising on the
QOF paper?

00:47:26.140 --> 00:47:33.600
Because if not, may I invite the Board, please,
to note the report that we've had and approve

00:47:33.600 --> 00:47:38.630
the reports, but also to approve the launch
of the engagement exercises on both the QOF

00:47:38.630 --> 00:47:39.630
and Digital First?

00:47:39.630 --> 00:47:41.500
Are we happy to do that?

00:47:41.500 --> 00:47:44.120
Good, thank you very much.

00:47:44.120 --> 00:47:50.360
We turn now to item six, which is around General
Practice Services Programme update.

00:47:50.360 --> 00:47:51.360
We have a paper.

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:57.500
I'm going to welcome Dominic Hardy to the
table, who is our Director of Primary Care

00:47:57.500 --> 00:47:58.500
delivery.

00:47:58.500 --> 00:48:03.160
And I think alongside Matthew, who will introduce
us to the paper.

00:48:03.160 --> 00:48:04.160
Matthew?

00:48:04.160 --> 00:48:06.180
Thank you very much, yes, and welcome to Dominic.

00:48:06.180 --> 00:48:11.660
I'd just say, in the context of the conversation
that we've just had about Digital First, this

00:48:11.660 --> 00:48:17.359
conversation is to say, there is a whole range
of piece - of work going on across primary

00:48:17.359 --> 00:48:23.120
care as the bedrock, and I would argue probably
the single biggest driver of why the NHS is

00:48:23.120 --> 00:48:26.830
the best-value health system in the world,
is our list-based general practice.

00:48:26.830 --> 00:48:31.730
And Dominic and his team have been working
for a number of years now to help alleviate

00:48:31.730 --> 00:48:38.420
the pressure in primary care and help move
it forward into what the future has, of which

00:48:38.420 --> 00:48:43.920
sliver of that is the digital piece, and so
much more of it is real people talking to

00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:45.330
real people every day.

00:48:45.330 --> 00:48:48.480
So, Dominic, why don't you talk us through
what's going on?

00:48:48.480 --> 00:48:51.859
Thanks, Matthew, and thank you for the opportunity,
Chair, to update on the progress.

00:48:51.859 --> 00:48:56.870
It's timely: we're over two years beyond the
publication of the General Practice Forward

00:48:56.870 --> 00:49:03.230
View, and we've made some significant progress
since April 2016, including in a number of

00:49:03.230 --> 00:49:04.230
key areas.

00:49:04.230 --> 00:49:09.590
We committed to significantly increasing investment
in general practice, and we are on track to

00:49:09.590 --> 00:49:17.820
deliver an additional 2.4 billion per year
by 2021, rising to a total of 12 billion.

00:49:17.820 --> 00:49:22.650
That investment is helping us deliver across
a range of other areas, including in extending

00:49:22.650 --> 00:49:28.300
access; well over half the population now
have access to general practice appointments

00:49:28.300 --> 00:49:30.510
at evenings and weekends.

00:49:30.510 --> 00:49:35.560
That's ahead of our target of around 40% by
the end of March earlier this year.

00:49:35.560 --> 00:49:40.350
That's allowed us to bring forward the timetable
for full roll-out of this, and so by October,

00:49:40.350 --> 00:49:45.490
100% of the population should be able to access
general practice appointments at evenings

00:49:45.490 --> 00:49:47.710
and at weekends.

00:49:47.710 --> 00:49:52.310
We've also sought to improve the general practice
infrastructure, and we've now completed almost

00:49:52.310 --> 00:49:59.080
1,000 projects to extend buildings and improve
them, and apply new technologies, including

00:49:59.080 --> 00:50:03.810
enable online consultations, as Matthew referred
to earlier.

00:50:03.810 --> 00:50:10.090
We've also been able to expand the general
practice workforce as a key component of sustaining

00:50:10.090 --> 00:50:12.980
general practice and renewing it for the future.

00:50:12.980 --> 00:50:22.140
We had a target to deliver an additional 5,000
non-GP staff by 2020, and we've already expanded

00:50:22.140 --> 00:50:24.790
that cadre of the workforce by 4,500.

00:50:24.790 --> 00:50:32.800
That includes almost another 500 full-time-equivalent
nurses in general practice, and over 700 clinical

00:50:32.800 --> 00:50:39.030
pharmacists, all of whom are enabling a more
multi-disciplinary approach and giving patients

00:50:39.030 --> 00:50:43.740
a wider range of staff who they can access
in general practice.

00:50:43.740 --> 00:50:48.680
We have a continuing challenge to deliver
the other workforce target of 5,000 additional

00:50:48.680 --> 00:50:51.200
doctors in general practice.

00:50:51.200 --> 00:50:56.720
Despite an increase in trainees, general practitioner
trainees, we have so far seen a net decrease

00:50:56.720 --> 00:50:58.420
in overall numbers.

00:50:58.420 --> 00:51:04.150
We continue to work hard on measures to recruit
GPs, including from overseas, and the recent

00:51:04.150 --> 00:51:08.830
government decision to lift the tier 2 visa
cap is going to help us accelerate that work

00:51:08.830 --> 00:51:11.720
programme significantly, we believe.

00:51:11.720 --> 00:51:16.200
And we work on retaining general practitioners
in the workforce, whether through addressing

00:51:16.200 --> 00:51:22.740
issues like rising indemnity costs and introducing
a state-backed scheme from next year, ideally,

00:51:22.740 --> 00:51:29.400
and also by bearing down on workload challenges
that undoubtedly press on general practice

00:51:29.400 --> 00:51:31.140
at the moment.

00:51:31.140 --> 00:51:35.420
Finally, looking to the future, we will not
only continue to deliver this programme with

00:51:35.420 --> 00:51:41.869
vigour, we will also particularly seek to
emphasise the establishment of primary care

00:51:41.869 --> 00:51:47.450
networks as a key means of enhancing resilience
in general practice and wider primary care,

00:51:47.450 --> 00:51:53.640
of enabling the deployment of a multi-disciplinary
workforce, and giving patients a wider range

00:51:53.640 --> 00:51:57.950
of practitioners and choice around how they
access general practice.

00:51:57.950 --> 00:52:02.850
So, I bring this report, Chair, for the Board
to note, and for discussion.

00:52:02.850 --> 00:52:07.160
Right, well, thank you very much, Dominic,
and actually, it's very pleasing to see the

00:52:07.160 --> 00:52:12.040
progress that's been made and on many fronts
ahead of forecast, so thank you for that and

00:52:12.040 --> 00:52:13.040
to your team.

00:52:13.040 --> 00:52:16.320
Let me invite the Board to pose any questions
or comments.

00:52:16.320 --> 00:52:17.320
Wendy?

00:52:17.320 --> 00:52:23.730
Dominic, we've obviously spent a lot of time
on this issue, and I think that there's a

00:52:23.730 --> 00:52:29.340
lot of good ideas that have been getting implemented,
which brings a lot of new momentum to it.

00:52:29.340 --> 00:52:32.220
The one - two issues I have, or two questions.

00:52:32.220 --> 00:52:36.609
One is a continued worry about our GPs and
the front-line staff, and the administrative

00:52:36.609 --> 00:52:40.450
burden that, though better, continues to exist
for them.

00:52:40.450 --> 00:52:46.320
Do you think we're doing all we can to really
leverage technology and give them the tools

00:52:46.320 --> 00:52:50.440
to be as helpful as possible in reducing that
burden?

00:52:50.440 --> 00:52:51.540
That's question one.

00:52:51.540 --> 00:52:57.310
And then question two, the primary care networks
are central to our future; they mean so much

00:52:57.310 --> 00:53:01.720
for our patients, and they will really be
a key element of us transforming care.

00:53:01.720 --> 00:53:07.280
Are we clear what the largest barriers are
to accelerating a greater and a faster shift

00:53:07.280 --> 00:53:09.609
to the primary care networks?

00:53:09.609 --> 00:53:17.600
So, to take those in turn, in terms of technologies
and how we apply those to ease workload burdens,

00:53:17.600 --> 00:53:18.780
we have made some inroads.

00:53:18.780 --> 00:53:25.160
So, the Time for Care programme has given
a really high number of practices now, several

00:53:25.160 --> 00:53:29.970
thousand, access to, for example, document
management systems, and I visited a practice

00:53:29.970 --> 00:53:35.601
in Lincoln yesterday who had made really good
use of that software and been able to see

00:53:35.601 --> 00:53:39.790
a really significant reduction in the workload
that they'd had as a practice.

00:53:39.790 --> 00:53:45.310
We've also worked with colleagues at the CQC
to try and ease some of the regulatory burden

00:53:45.310 --> 00:53:50.760
that practices face, and the work you've already
discussed around online consultations is equally

00:53:50.760 --> 00:53:54.400
helping some practices address that kind of
workload issue.

00:53:54.400 --> 00:53:58.700
I've heard about practices in Sidcup, for
example, that I visited on Friday, who've

00:53:58.700 --> 00:54:03.310
made really significant progress even in a
few weeks and months in applying that kind

00:54:03.310 --> 00:54:04.310
of technology.

00:54:04.310 --> 00:54:09.200
There's undoubtedly a lot more to do, and
the remaining period of this programme is

00:54:09.200 --> 00:54:12.520
squarely going to focus on that continued
effort.

00:54:12.520 --> 00:54:20.160
For primary care networks, the biggest barriers
are not normally around enthusiasm.

00:54:20.160 --> 00:54:26.440
So, we've worked with hundreds, thousands
of practices who are pursuing this course

00:54:26.440 --> 00:54:27.440
with vigour.

00:54:27.440 --> 00:54:34.100
Often, the need is for some headroom among
practices to think about how they like to

00:54:34.100 --> 00:54:37.930
collaborate more effectively and do that to
best effect on behalf of their patients and

00:54:37.930 --> 00:54:39.280
the staff they employ.

00:54:39.280 --> 00:54:45.380
So, creating some of the capacity, to Ian's
earlier point, through people that we can

00:54:45.380 --> 00:54:50.950
help provide, and through financial resources,
can give them some of that thinking space

00:54:50.950 --> 00:54:53.860
to make the changes that they need and, frankly,
want to make.

00:54:53.860 --> 00:54:58.849
But that is something that, as I say, we need
to continue to address over the forthcoming

00:54:58.849 --> 00:55:00.260
couple of years.

00:55:00.260 --> 00:55:03.520
Richard and David.

00:55:03.520 --> 00:55:11.340
So, on this, there's lot of - there's been
lots of ambition, there's been lots of progress

00:55:11.340 --> 00:55:13.740
in lots of areas, so I think that's really
good.

00:55:13.740 --> 00:55:18.480
The one area, quite clearly, that stands out
from this is on the number of doctors working

00:55:18.480 --> 00:55:21.400
in GP practice.

00:55:21.400 --> 00:55:28.760
And I guess my question for you, Dominic,
is: what do we understand about the drivers

00:55:28.760 --> 00:55:35.320
of the lower level of retention, and to what
extent does that interact with the work that

00:55:35.320 --> 00:55:40.030
Ian was talking about a few minutes ago on
contract reform?

00:55:40.030 --> 00:55:43.080
Just what's our understanding on what's happening
to retention and why?

00:55:43.080 --> 00:55:50.290
So, successive surveys and other research,
Richard, points to a few key drivers, whether

00:55:50.290 --> 00:55:58.050
it's around workload pressures and increasing
demands, not just by age, but also by complexity

00:55:58.050 --> 00:56:03.520
and acuity from the patients presenting.

00:56:03.520 --> 00:56:09.170
Sometimes, through fringe issues like rising
indemnity costs, but also because GPs at the

00:56:09.170 --> 00:56:16.359
higher end of their experience have generally
filled their lifetime allowance in their pension,

00:56:16.359 --> 00:56:20.990
and have almost an opportunity to retire early.

00:56:20.990 --> 00:56:27.369
And so to that extent, we seek to address
each of those issues and retain as many GPs

00:56:27.369 --> 00:56:31.440
in the workforce as we can, particularly at
the upper end, where often, GPs are at their

00:56:31.440 --> 00:56:32.560
most productive.

00:56:32.560 --> 00:56:38.840
Sometimes, that can involve seeking to retain
those more experienced GPs for fewer sessions

00:56:38.840 --> 00:56:39.840
per week.

00:56:39.840 --> 00:56:44.950
That can even mean them operating in a floating
capacity across a number of practices, and

00:56:44.950 --> 00:56:49.730
we're working with all of the STPs and ICSs
across the country to support them and resource

00:56:49.730 --> 00:56:54.880
them, to deploy exactly those kinds of schemes
so that we do secure as many of the more experienced

00:56:54.880 --> 00:56:56.630
GPs for as long as possible.

00:56:56.630 --> 00:57:03.140
And in terms of workload, some of the work
we're doing that I described in response to

00:57:03.140 --> 00:57:08.050
Wendy's question is exactly about that: helping
practices, individually and together, bear

00:57:08.050 --> 00:57:15.369
down on the root causes on that by expanding
their workforce, and using other skills across

00:57:15.369 --> 00:57:20.530
the team to supplement the GP capacity that
we've got, as well as getting more productive

00:57:20.530 --> 00:57:24.520
by using technologies, for example, to enhance
productivity and workflow.

00:57:24.520 --> 00:57:32.830
So, on those ones where we can identify particularly
things like the indemnity issue, we've got

00:57:32.830 --> 00:57:38.400
work in progress on, things where it's to
do with workload, you can do things around

00:57:38.400 --> 00:57:41.890
sort of reducing bureaucracy, and the other
things we would target.

00:57:41.890 --> 00:57:45.820
I guess - and it's one of the reasons I asked
about this - the lifetime allowance issue

00:57:45.820 --> 00:57:50.550
on the pensions, everything I've heard has
been quite a big issue.

00:57:50.550 --> 00:57:56.070
There's not really a lot you can do about
that, or is there?

00:57:56.070 --> 00:58:02.780
I mean, are there different ways of approaching
this that get you into that, or not?

00:58:02.780 --> 00:58:08.170
So, we have had a number of discussions with
Department of Health and Social Care colleagues,

00:58:08.170 --> 00:58:14.050
and we continue to pursue those, but we are,
as you imply, part of a wider public sector

00:58:14.050 --> 00:58:19.230
picture here, so we recognise that this is
a wider discussion that needs to happen at

00:58:19.230 --> 00:58:20.510
the centre of government.

00:58:20.510 --> 00:58:25.130
And the thing we're finding that has been
- that is most effective and most transformational

00:58:25.130 --> 00:58:31.910
is the combination of the additional non-GP
clinical workforce, and where areas have rethought

00:58:31.910 --> 00:58:32.910
how they're working.

00:58:32.910 --> 00:58:36.670
So, we do see across the country practices,
particularly where they're coming together

00:58:36.670 --> 00:58:40.740
into networks and you're thinking bigger than
a practice by practice, how do you actually

00:58:40.740 --> 00:58:47.020
use the combination of new clinical skills
coming in, working as part of a team with

00:58:47.020 --> 00:58:56.250
GPs, and the use of technology, and the combination
of our best examples, to change the working

00:58:56.250 --> 00:58:57.830
life of GPs?

00:58:57.830 --> 00:59:02.540
And we're getting some tremendously positive
feedback from a whole host of parts of the

00:59:02.540 --> 00:59:04.280
country now who've gone through that work.

00:59:04.280 --> 00:59:05.540
Is that fair, Dom?

00:59:05.540 --> 00:59:06.540
It's absolutely right.

00:59:06.540 --> 00:59:13.840
The particular challenge we've got is to shift
from individual practices, of whom I can give

00:59:13.840 --> 00:59:19.260
you countless examples, who tackled the kind
of challenge of retiring GPs and losing partners

00:59:19.260 --> 00:59:25.510
from their practice, and that becoming something
that happens in a more scaled, wholesale way.

00:59:25.510 --> 00:59:31.859
Because if we rely on 7,500 no doubt ingenious
practices each solving these problems themselves,

00:59:31.859 --> 00:59:34.430
that's a very slow way to get to where we
need to be.

00:59:34.430 --> 00:59:40.260
If we can do that through primary care networks
and versions thereof who can do that at greater

00:59:40.260 --> 00:59:45.570
scale in a more collaborative way, we expect
that we could deliver those kinds of changes

00:59:45.570 --> 00:59:50.770
and create a thriving general practice sector
more quickly.

00:59:50.770 --> 00:59:54.070
David and Steve and Moira.

00:59:54.070 --> 00:59:59.920
So, Dominic, last time I asked you this question,
you said the answer was the visa problem,

00:59:59.920 --> 01:00:02.030
which you've solved, so well done.

01:00:02.030 --> 01:00:04.150
I'll give you the credit.

01:00:04.150 --> 01:00:08.910
But actually, boards are here I think to help
as well as to oversee and challenge, so my

01:00:08.910 --> 01:00:18.270
question is - and it may be that it's a short
answer - if you had a fairy - sort of a magic

01:00:18.270 --> 01:00:27.599
wand, and you could pull - do one thing that
would improve the effectiveness of your programme

01:00:27.599 --> 01:00:34.450
and the workload - I'm particularly looking
at workload and retention of GPs - where this

01:00:34.450 --> 01:00:40.310
Board could help, either directly or through
its convening or its sort of campaigning type

01:00:40.310 --> 01:00:42.400
of activities, what would that be?

01:00:42.400 --> 01:00:46.340
What would be the one thing that would make
a really big difference?

01:00:46.340 --> 01:00:49.890
So, I think there are a number of answers
I could give.

01:00:49.890 --> 01:00:54.609
One that I am going to give that I've seen
in action recently is the wider application

01:00:54.609 --> 01:00:59.930
and speedier application of technology, and
the support for practices to optimise that.

01:00:59.930 --> 01:01:05.359
So, where I've seen the deployment of online
consultation systems, it has had a transformational

01:01:05.359 --> 01:01:11.390
effect on the ability of those practices to
cope with the day-to-day workload pressures

01:01:11.390 --> 01:01:17.420
across general practitioners, practice nurses,
clinical pharmacists, and I believe that if

01:01:17.420 --> 01:01:22.180
we could seriously accelerate that, individual
practices would find their daily lives considerably

01:01:22.180 --> 01:01:23.210
easier.

01:01:23.210 --> 01:01:26.500
So, what would you need to allow that to happen?

01:01:26.500 --> 01:01:28.849
Is it sort of more resource?

01:01:28.849 --> 01:01:31.880
Is it implementation skills?

01:01:31.880 --> 01:01:34.920
Is it sort of - what would it be?

01:01:34.920 --> 01:01:38.770
So, I think there's - there are a combination
of things.

01:01:38.770 --> 01:01:44.610
The funding is there, and in the paper we
talk about the £45 million across three years.

01:01:44.610 --> 01:01:50.340
Probably the deficit we've got at the moment
is almost the sales pitch, if I can put it

01:01:50.340 --> 01:01:54.840
as crudely as that, to practices about the
benefits and a corresponding belief about

01:01:54.840 --> 01:01:57.150
the benefits that can accrue.

01:01:57.150 --> 01:02:02.570
And also then some capacity, whether through
our own local teams, or clinical commissioning

01:02:02.570 --> 01:02:08.700
groups, to get shoulder to shoulder with practices
and help them and the suppliers of these products

01:02:08.700 --> 01:02:11.200
properly embed them and optimise them.

01:02:11.200 --> 01:02:17.480
So, given that answer, I wonder, Wendy, whether
through the Commissioning Committee or whether,

01:02:17.480 --> 01:02:22.050
Malcolm, through the Board - because this
is - you know, we've all said GPs are the

01:02:22.050 --> 01:02:25.220
bedrock of the Service, absolutely agree with
that.

01:02:25.220 --> 01:02:30.869
We all know we've got both short-term and
long-term workforce challenges, and we appear,

01:02:30.869 --> 01:02:33.470
from Dominic's skill and judgement, to have
a sort of something that we could think about

01:02:33.470 --> 01:02:34.470
accelerating.

01:02:34.470 --> 01:02:42.560
So, I guess my question is, to Malcolm, to
Wendy: can we not as a Board encourage that

01:02:42.560 --> 01:02:48.779
through suggesting that we should ask Dominic
to come back at some stage and say, -This

01:02:48.779 --> 01:02:53.690
is what we're now going to do, and recognising,
Simon and Matthew, and others, supporting

01:02:53.690 --> 01:02:56.330
by making necessary prioritisation decisions?

01:02:56.330 --> 01:02:58.810
To raid Mr Baumann's pockets, as a leaving
present?

01:02:58.810 --> 01:03:00.450
Why don't we put that as high priority?

01:03:00.450 --> 01:03:03.020
Wendy, if you're happy, I think that comes
through Commissioning Committee; it should

01:03:03.020 --> 01:03:04.020
come back to Board.

01:03:04.020 --> 01:03:05.250
Yeah, and we are on that.

01:03:05.250 --> 01:03:07.960
That was a little hidden news behind my question.

01:03:07.960 --> 01:03:14.630
Because I think that is so central, and I
think that's well taken and en route, as well.

01:03:14.630 --> 01:03:18.580
It strikes me, Simon, as something where,
you know, we could make a material difference

01:03:18.580 --> 01:03:20.339
in a very short period of time, putting pressure
on.

01:03:20.339 --> 01:03:23.420
Right, well, we will take that forward as
an action item.

01:03:23.420 --> 01:03:25.950
Let me invite Steve, Moira and Jane.

01:03:25.950 --> 01:03:30.859
Well, it was really to support and emphasise
the point that both Matthew and Dom made in

01:03:30.859 --> 01:03:35.240
answer to Richard's question around what were
the drivers for poorer retention.

01:03:35.240 --> 01:03:41.130
And I believe, and my experience is that scale
offers a solution to many of those issues,

01:03:41.130 --> 01:03:45.460
and I think that's why the development of
primary care networks, which you mentioned

01:03:45.460 --> 01:03:50.599
at the start, is so important, because that
gives a flexibility within a group of practices

01:03:50.599 --> 01:03:58.690
to deploy additional staff, to offer a flexibility
of return to work, which is not so easy to

01:03:58.690 --> 01:04:01.490
do in smaller practices.

01:04:01.490 --> 01:04:02.900
Thanks, Malcolm.

01:04:02.900 --> 01:04:08.380
Lots of good things to report, Dominic, but
obviously, Richard's drawn attention to the

01:04:08.380 --> 01:04:13.220
decrease in the number of full-time-equivalent
doctors.

01:04:13.220 --> 01:04:20.520
Generally, under-doctoring has impacted more
on more deprived areas, and we've - my colleague

01:04:20.520 --> 01:04:25.980
here on my right has regularly drawn attention
to the inverse care law.

01:04:25.980 --> 01:04:32.581
And I wonder whether there is any - with the
things that are working well, whether there

01:04:32.581 --> 01:04:41.349
is any change to that impact, disproportionate
impact, on deprived areas, or is it still

01:04:41.349 --> 01:04:43.369
- is it getting worse?

01:04:43.369 --> 01:04:50.460
So, we've had some success, Moira, in being
able to deploy GP trainees to areas of the

01:04:50.460 --> 01:04:54.010
country that have found it historically hard
to fill their training places.

01:04:54.010 --> 01:05:02.260
We've paid a targeted, enhanced recruitment
pot of money to trainees of £20,000 a time

01:05:02.260 --> 01:05:07.160
to go to those areas, and there is some evidence
via Health Education England that trainees

01:05:07.160 --> 01:05:08.940
have then stayed in that area.

01:05:08.940 --> 01:05:13.630
So, there is some soft evidence, I would say,
that we're starting to get to grips with all

01:05:13.630 --> 01:05:22.420
of that, but we continue to need practices
and their trainees, and CCGs and local offices,

01:05:22.420 --> 01:05:27.980
to work incredibly hard to ensure that those
trainees are offered opportunities in the

01:05:27.980 --> 01:05:29.570
patch in which they train and then stay.

01:05:29.570 --> 01:05:35.480
I was in Lincolnshire yesterday, as I said,
and they clearly have a good deal of work

01:05:35.480 --> 01:05:36.480
to do to ensure that happens.

01:05:36.480 --> 01:05:41.140
They're clear what needs to be done, but the
scale of the challenge is considerable, because

01:05:41.140 --> 01:05:46.670
this is not something that we've tackled effectively,
being able to secure our trainees full stop,

01:05:46.670 --> 01:05:49.060
and then in the areas in which they train.

01:05:49.060 --> 01:05:53.520
The opening of five new medical schools, including
one in the University of Lincoln, will help

01:05:53.520 --> 01:05:58.390
that, but it will still need the whole of
the system to work together to best effect

01:05:58.390 --> 01:06:04.700
to get the kind of impact that we're looking
for and bear down on the under-doctored challenge

01:06:04.700 --> 01:06:08.640
that we've got and the consequent inequalities
of access.

01:06:08.640 --> 01:06:09.730
Jane?

01:06:09.730 --> 01:06:14.270
I think I just wanted to build on what Dominic
was saying about GPs.

01:06:14.270 --> 01:06:19.200
I think - I completely support the plans and
the actions that we've got in place, but general

01:06:19.200 --> 01:06:26.450
practice is wider than GPs, and we know from
experience that actually using and having

01:06:26.450 --> 01:06:32.980
advanced paramedics, physiotherapists, mental
health therapists, clinical pharmacists, nurses,

01:06:32.980 --> 01:06:35.900
working in general practice can actually have
a very positive effect.

01:06:35.900 --> 01:06:40.640
So, I think as well as spending quite a significant
amount of our time looking at how we can support

01:06:40.640 --> 01:06:47.750
the GPs, and also recruit and retain more,
I think it's - we - it would be good for us

01:06:47.750 --> 01:06:53.030
to look at the wider workforce, and we can
address some of those shortages much more

01:06:53.030 --> 01:06:54.450
quickly.

01:06:54.450 --> 01:06:57.140
We are doing work around general practice
nursing, as you know.

01:06:57.140 --> 01:07:02.580
It is a significant area where we will - we
are facing a significant reduction of those

01:07:02.580 --> 01:07:08.530
nurses in the future, and there are some very
straightforward things which may take a little

01:07:08.530 --> 01:07:12.940
bit of time to get through, but things like
terms and conditions, employment models, etc.,

01:07:12.940 --> 01:07:18.810
are very, very different across the country,
and I do think we've got some opportunities

01:07:18.810 --> 01:07:24.000
to do some very good work, and it will make
a big difference to the lives of GPs and patients

01:07:24.000 --> 01:07:28.770
if there is better access to advanced practitioners
who are not just doctors.

01:07:28.770 --> 01:07:34.300
I think on that note, if Suzanne Rastrick
were here, who heads up our Allied Health

01:07:34.300 --> 01:07:36.500
Professionals
That's why I mentioned AHPs[?].

01:07:36.500 --> 01:07:41.580
She would be very strong on that, and also
emphasising the fact that so many allied health

01:07:41.580 --> 01:07:46.770
professionals are frustrated by the limits
to what they're permitted to do, and by the

01:07:46.770 --> 01:07:51.750
capacity to grow into the - much more into
the sorts of roles that you've just outlined.

01:07:51.750 --> 01:07:52.780
Steve?

01:07:52.780 --> 01:07:56.660
And then not just Suzanne, because if Keith
Ridge was here, he would say the same about

01:07:56.660 --> 01:08:00.610
pharmacists, which Jane has mentioned, and
we are deliberately putting more pharmacists

01:08:00.610 --> 01:08:05.430
into general practice to deal with issues
such as polypharmacy, to think about how we

01:08:05.430 --> 01:08:09.359
increase rates of anti-coagulation amongst
atrial - patients with atrial fibrillation

01:08:09.359 --> 01:08:10.480
to prevent strokes.

01:08:10.480 --> 01:08:14.330
So, I absolutely agree with Jane: this is
around the primary care workforce and not

01:08:14.330 --> 01:08:16.319
just around the general practitioners.

01:08:16.319 --> 01:08:21.910
And of course, much of that engagement with
allied health professionals and other professionals

01:08:21.910 --> 01:08:29.349
is, to some extent, a function of scale, and
I think the GP networks are an enabler to

01:08:29.349 --> 01:08:34.489
be able to employ a broader workforce across
a larger population.

01:08:34.489 --> 01:08:40.819
So, are there further issues to raise with
Matthew and Dom?

01:08:40.819 --> 01:08:41.819
No?

01:08:41.819 --> 01:08:42.819
Well, thank you.

01:08:42.819 --> 01:08:43.819
By the way, really good.

01:08:43.819 --> 01:08:49.750
I think you get a sense that the Board would
really welcome further updates and developments

01:08:49.750 --> 01:08:50.750
on this.

01:08:50.750 --> 01:08:52.019
We're taking a very close interest in it.

01:08:52.019 --> 01:08:57.219
It's being done, I think, very carefully within
the Commissioning Committee, but this is work

01:08:57.219 --> 01:09:00.599
that deserves a broader discussion around
the boardroom table.

01:09:00.599 --> 01:09:03.819
We've got some specific items that we will
take back through Commissioning Committee

01:09:03.819 --> 01:09:07.140
and work closely with you and the team, and
Wendy I know will pick those up.

01:09:07.140 --> 01:09:12.270
But on behalf of the Board, thank you for
all the work, Dom.

01:09:12.270 --> 01:09:13.270
Thank you.

01:09:13.270 --> 01:09:14.270
Okay.

01:09:14.270 --> 01:09:17.679
May we then move on to the report that we
have from the Empowering People and Communities

01:09:17.679 --> 01:09:18.679
Task Force?

01:09:18.679 --> 01:09:21.170
This is the second progress report.

01:09:21.170 --> 01:09:25.289
Victor is chair of this, but I'm going to
invite Jane to kick off, and then we'll move

01:09:25.289 --> 01:09:26.290
on from there.

01:09:26.290 --> 01:09:27.290
Jane?

01:09:27.290 --> 01:09:28.290
Thank you.

01:09:28.290 --> 01:09:30.109
I should welcome Neil to
Yes, being joined by Neil.

01:09:30.109 --> 01:09:31.109
Thank you.

01:09:31.109 --> 01:09:34.709
So, I'm just going to say very little, actually,
because I think it's probably more important

01:09:34.709 --> 01:09:39.440
that we listen and hear from Victor, but this
is the second update on the work of this Task

01:09:39.440 --> 01:09:40.440
Force.

01:09:40.440 --> 01:09:41.440
The Board will remember it's time-limited.

01:09:41.440 --> 01:09:47.619
We set it up to last until the end of December,
very - with some very specific objectives

01:09:47.619 --> 01:09:53.699
and work, and this report just provides an
update on sort of three key areas.

01:09:53.699 --> 01:09:58.849
And I think probably one of the most important
ones of those is our - the new public participation

01:09:58.849 --> 01:10:03.349
dashboard, but it also talks more about the
work we're doing to improve our partnerships

01:10:03.349 --> 01:10:08.409
with the voluntary sector, but also lists
some of the improvements that we feel we need

01:10:08.409 --> 01:10:10.949
to make and some of the objectives we've got
to do that.

01:10:10.949 --> 01:10:12.469
So, it is making progress.

01:10:12.469 --> 01:10:17.280
We are finding very good links with our national
priorities, and it's really putting a good

01:10:17.280 --> 01:10:18.409
focus on that.

01:10:18.409 --> 01:10:21.389
So, I'll hand over to Victor, I guess, who
I think would be able to - who chairs the

01:10:21.389 --> 01:10:23.530
Task Force and will no doubt be able to tell
us a lot more.

01:10:23.530 --> 01:10:25.059
Yeah, well, I'm happy to say a couple of things.

01:10:25.059 --> 01:10:30.579
The first thing I should say is to - I should
thank all the people that have come to the

01:10:30.579 --> 01:10:35.489
sessions, because the way we operate the Task
Force is quite different: we engage a number

01:10:35.489 --> 01:10:40.030
of patient groups and interest groups around
the areas that we've looked at - cancer and

01:10:40.030 --> 01:10:46.800
mental health - and things like coproduction,
and I think it's important to thank them,

01:10:46.800 --> 01:10:51.940
because some of the insights have been directly
as a result of their input, so I'd like to

01:10:51.940 --> 01:10:52.940
do that.

01:10:52.940 --> 01:10:57.760
Of course, Michelle might want to say a few
words, as well, as my co-chair; basically

01:10:57.760 --> 01:11:00.309
brilliant job, thank you, Michelle.

01:11:00.309 --> 01:11:05.139
The dashboard is - it paints a picture, actually,
which we should be reasonably pleased with,

01:11:05.139 --> 01:11:12.739
you know, it's - in terms of our - the Board's
legal obligations under the Act.

01:11:12.739 --> 01:11:17.639
Where we are not doing so good, I would surmise
it's because we haven't accepted or haven't

01:11:17.639 --> 01:11:22.650
engaged fully with the notion of coproduction
in the delivery of health and social care

01:11:22.650 --> 01:11:26.969
in these areas, and using the systems that
we've got in those areas to do that.

01:11:26.969 --> 01:11:34.550
And in light of the ten-year plan, we do know
that coproduction is more likely to obtain

01:11:34.550 --> 01:11:41.170
more sustainable health and care services
and, indeed, better financial outcomes.

01:11:41.170 --> 01:11:48.199
Moving on, we have across the work that we've
done in cancer and mental health, without

01:11:48.199 --> 01:11:52.570
going into a lot of detail because it's in
the report, the thing that strikes me across

01:11:52.570 --> 01:11:57.690
both of those is the notion of inequalities
and inequity, which I'm grateful the Board

01:11:57.690 --> 01:12:01.989
have taken on some of those insights.

01:12:01.989 --> 01:12:06.289
And I'm also grateful for the leaders of those
programmes who have responded well to the

01:12:06.289 --> 01:12:10.760
letters describing those insights to the committees.

01:12:10.760 --> 01:12:20.310
I am still - personally, I guess, but I think
Michelle would back this up - still concerned

01:12:20.310 --> 01:12:25.559
about the responses in-programme to some of
the observations we've made about cancer.

01:12:25.559 --> 01:12:31.079
I'm really pleased that in mental health,
for instance, we have a date for the production

01:12:31.079 --> 01:12:37.360
of data on a minority ethnic experience of
mental health programmes to date.

01:12:37.360 --> 01:12:41.380
I think that is quite important.

01:12:41.380 --> 01:12:50.219
Moving forward, we will be producing a more
detailed recommendation report to the Board

01:12:50.219 --> 01:12:56.260
on the relationship with the voluntary and
community sector.

01:12:56.260 --> 01:13:03.599
We have quite high ambitions for the relationship
between the NHS and the voluntary and community

01:13:03.599 --> 01:13:07.059
sector, because we believe - and the sector
is telling us - that they could do a lot more

01:13:07.059 --> 01:13:12.989
to deliver the ambitions within the plan,
so we'll be coming forward with specific recommendations

01:13:12.989 --> 01:13:16.110
that lead on from the notes in this report.

01:13:16.110 --> 01:13:24.199
We'll also be looking at frailty, and I can
thank Noel for raising this issue quite some

01:13:24.199 --> 01:13:33.369
time ago, actually, but we'll be looking at
frailty and the use of patient experience

01:13:33.369 --> 01:13:39.190
data in strategy decision making, because
we've got a lot of data; the question is,

01:13:39.190 --> 01:13:41.989
how do we turn it into information that helps
treatment decision-making?

01:13:41.989 --> 01:13:48.679
And looking forward, we have some options
as to what we might look at, but we may well

01:13:48.679 --> 01:13:55.139
revisit the cancer work and look at - or look
at primary care or learning disabilities.

01:13:55.139 --> 01:13:59.099
And given that the Task Force will finish
in December, we'll also be thinking about

01:13:59.099 --> 01:14:04.119
what the legacy is and how it plays into the
new structures and ideas around the ten-year

01:14:04.119 --> 01:14:06.060
plan and NHSE and NHSI.

01:14:06.060 --> 01:14:11.570
I don't know whether Michelle would like to
add anything to what I've said?

01:14:11.570 --> 01:14:12.570
No.

01:14:12.570 --> 01:14:15.460
Or indeed, Neil.

01:14:15.460 --> 01:14:17.789
Congratulations, again, on the gong.

01:14:17.789 --> 01:14:18.789
Thank you, Victor.

01:14:18.789 --> 01:14:23.090
I think, just to talk to some - a couple of
the improvement priorities, I think, which

01:14:23.090 --> 01:14:29.190
came through both for NHS England and for
CCGs, one of which was around telling people

01:14:29.190 --> 01:14:31.320
what has changed as a result of their involvement.

01:14:31.320 --> 01:14:36.709
And so generally, I think there's a story
being told around the practice, and it is

01:14:36.709 --> 01:14:38.610
actually very good.

01:14:38.610 --> 01:14:41.849
This is now part of business as usual, but
we haven't done as much as we should to tell

01:14:41.849 --> 01:14:45.949
people what's different as a result, and that's
one of the things that's important in building

01:14:45.949 --> 01:14:48.730
people's confidence and engagement continuing.

01:14:48.730 --> 01:14:54.059
And I think a second improvement priority,
as Victor has said, is to make sure that we

01:14:54.059 --> 01:14:58.789
are more proactive in listening to voices
which are otherwise seldom heard, and involving

01:14:58.789 --> 01:15:02.840
them in our discussions where proper, and
we're not doing as well as we would like to

01:15:02.840 --> 01:15:03.840
be doing.

01:15:03.840 --> 01:15:09.550
But on the whole, I think, Victor, I agree
that the story is a pretty positive one, and

01:15:09.550 --> 01:15:13.579
we will have new data published soon, and
we can see how it will have changed year on

01:15:13.579 --> 01:15:14.579
year.

01:15:14.579 --> 01:15:15.579
Great, thank you.

01:15:15.579 --> 01:15:18.349
Let me open it up then for discussion.

01:15:18.349 --> 01:15:19.940
Where shall we start?

01:15:19.940 --> 01:15:21.340
With Noel, please.

01:15:21.340 --> 01:15:29.260
I'm delighted, Victor, you said we have high
ambitions for strengthening our engagement

01:15:29.260 --> 01:15:33.070
with the voluntary sector, and it seems to
me extremely important we do so in areas like

01:15:33.070 --> 01:15:38.070
ageing and mental health, and diabetes.

01:15:38.070 --> 01:15:43.349
But I'm just interested in - so I can see
that we're coming forward with recommendations

01:15:43.349 --> 01:15:46.679
on how to strengthen that in the next phase
of the work.

01:15:46.679 --> 01:15:52.460
I'm just interested in what you've found in
this work were the principal reasons why we

01:15:52.460 --> 01:15:59.119
have not engaged so strongly in the past in
leveraging the power of the third sector to

01:15:59.119 --> 01:16:02.070
help us in some of our delivery challenges.

01:16:02.070 --> 01:16:07.360
Do you want me to answer that, to respond?

01:16:07.360 --> 01:16:11.619
I mean, firstly, I think the experience is
patchy.

01:16:11.619 --> 01:16:15.170
Certainly in mental health, there is a good
story.

01:16:15.170 --> 01:16:20.570
The development of the mental health plan
was done not only with 20,000 engagements

01:16:20.570 --> 01:16:27.170
or contacts with people, but also with a number
of high-profile voluntary organisations.

01:16:27.170 --> 01:16:28.800
The same can be said for cancer.

01:16:28.800 --> 01:16:36.489
The question might be, to what extent have
we structured our engagement with the voluntary

01:16:36.489 --> 01:16:43.110
sector to ensure that seldom-heard voices
and seldom-heard members of that sector actually

01:16:43.110 --> 01:16:44.790
impact the programmes?

01:16:44.790 --> 01:16:49.550
And I think we've got some way to go on that,
which is why we've been talking about - and

01:16:49.550 --> 01:16:56.000
it's been a real debate - about what principles
might drive the next step in that.

01:16:56.000 --> 01:17:01.150
The second challenge I think we have is the
go-to points.

01:17:01.150 --> 01:17:09.199
So, there are lots of players in the sector,
both individuals and organisations.

01:17:09.199 --> 01:17:13.639
Who do they go to in NHS England, or NHSI
for that matter?

01:17:13.639 --> 01:17:18.619
And how do we organise ourselves to get them
to actually organise themselves, so that we

01:17:18.619 --> 01:17:23.510
have single points of contact on key strategic
issues?

01:17:23.510 --> 01:17:29.789
And how does that drive on - help us move
the needle in the areas of our concern?

01:17:29.789 --> 01:17:34.619
Because the voluntary sector is often seen
as a nice-to-have.

01:17:34.619 --> 01:17:39.130
It's a bit like kissing babies; nobody's going
to be against it, but it doesn't actually

01:17:39.130 --> 01:17:46.960
- it's not actually seen as a measurable partner
in the way that we do strategy, and that's

01:17:46.960 --> 01:17:48.400
missing out.

01:17:48.400 --> 01:17:53.630
So, I think those are the things that we need
to focus on.

01:17:53.630 --> 01:17:59.539
I would say that if we're going to move the
needle on inequalities and inequity - and

01:17:59.539 --> 01:18:04.590
I use those two words deliberately; they're
not the same thing - then in key areas of

01:18:04.590 --> 01:18:13.000
the country, we are going to have to partner
with both the - when I say voluntary sector,

01:18:13.000 --> 01:18:17.840
I'm being careful, because the social enterprise
sector and the charity sector are not the

01:18:17.840 --> 01:18:18.840
same thing.

01:18:18.840 --> 01:18:23.409
But we are going to have to partner with not-for-dividend,
for want of a better term, and not-for-profit

01:18:23.409 --> 01:18:26.829
organisations in a much clearer way than we
are doing at the moment.

01:18:26.829 --> 01:18:29.270
That's very helpful, thank you.

01:18:29.270 --> 01:18:30.270
Michelle?

01:18:30.270 --> 01:18:35.530
Just to build on Victor's points, what we
also see, Noel, is a significant diversity

01:18:35.530 --> 01:18:37.730
of relationship at a local level.

01:18:37.730 --> 01:18:43.959
So, the King's Fund published a report recently
which described those very different experiences

01:18:43.959 --> 01:18:49.550
where the relationship and contribution of
the voluntary sector is valued at a local

01:18:49.550 --> 01:18:52.400
level, there are very mature relationships.

01:18:52.400 --> 01:18:58.099
And as Neil pointed out, some of the work
in a number of local areas is really excellent,

01:18:58.099 --> 01:19:01.719
and genuinely partners in the transformation
care.

01:19:01.719 --> 01:19:09.360
But it certainly isn't that across the country,
and building that good practice locally is

01:19:09.360 --> 01:19:10.360
critical.

01:19:10.360 --> 01:19:17.769
The other points to pull out is, one, really
looking forward to the work that Jane Sanderson

01:19:17.769 --> 01:19:25.199
will bring forward on personalised care, and
I think that is a particularly important area

01:19:25.199 --> 01:19:29.289
of work where there's great interest within
the voluntary sector.

01:19:29.289 --> 01:19:36.280
And also, looking at how the voluntary and
community sector can work as part of the embryonic

01:19:36.280 --> 01:19:38.809
proposals for an assembly.

01:19:38.809 --> 01:19:44.619
So, there are a number of moving feasts where
there's some great opportunities to continue

01:19:44.619 --> 01:19:45.619
to make progress.

01:19:45.619 --> 01:19:48.030
We are making recommendations on both those
things.

01:19:48.030 --> 01:19:51.790
I would just say one last thing, if I may,
Chair, which is that in the development of

01:19:51.790 --> 01:19:59.260
ISCs and STPs, there is a risk that the good
work that we've done isn't embedded in the

01:19:59.260 --> 01:20:05.020
way that we do population health, and it's
just a question for how we embed those new

01:20:05.020 --> 01:20:10.510
structures of population health and ensure
that we take those learnings with us.

01:20:10.510 --> 01:20:14.630
Good, thank you, Victor, Michelle.

01:20:14.630 --> 01:20:17.099
Other comments, questions?

01:20:17.099 --> 01:20:22.920
That point will need to be picked up, of course,
about how these processes are embedded as

01:20:22.920 --> 01:20:27.539
we develop the STPs and particularly, again,
the ICSs.

01:20:27.539 --> 01:20:29.329
Right.

01:20:29.329 --> 01:20:32.659
I detect that we've had a very good presentation.

01:20:32.659 --> 01:20:38.329
Thanks again to Neil for your work on this,
and to Victor and Michelle for your co-chairing

01:20:38.329 --> 01:20:39.420
of the Task Force.

01:20:39.420 --> 01:20:45.570
And we will, of course, have another report
at the end of the year, in which we - it will

01:20:45.570 --> 01:20:49.670
wind up, but as you suggested, Michelle, also
leading into thinking about the assembly and

01:20:49.670 --> 01:20:51.869
the function of the assembly.

01:20:51.869 --> 01:20:56.989
So, it strikes me that there is a good coincidence
of timing between the Task Force and the next

01:20:56.989 --> 01:20:59.539
stages of this important part of the agenda.

01:20:59.539 --> 01:21:02.610
Okay, well, thank you, everybody, for that.

01:21:02.610 --> 01:21:08.260
We're going to move now on to item eight in
the agenda, which is our report on next steps

01:21:08.260 --> 01:21:10.130
on the NHS Five Year Forward View.

01:21:10.130 --> 01:21:12.150
What are we doing, Matthew?

01:21:12.150 --> 01:21:13.150
How are we doing, Matthew?

01:21:13.150 --> 01:21:18.019
We're doing a lot, you'll be pleased to know.

01:21:18.019 --> 01:21:23.869
Just to run you through some of the highlights
from the report.

01:21:23.869 --> 01:21:34.639
Urgent and emergency care, A&amp;E performance
was improved to over 90% last month, and looking

01:21:34.639 --> 01:21:43.170
across the wider pathway, we saw the number
of delayed transfers of care has fallen to

01:21:43.170 --> 01:21:50.130
- it's now 18% lower than it was at the same
time last year, and that 1,812 fewer DTOCs

01:21:50.130 --> 01:21:57.429
than when we set the baseline back in May
2017, which is a tremendous effort by the

01:21:57.429 --> 01:22:04.420
NHS and their partners in local government,
and we continue to pressure - press on reducing

01:22:04.420 --> 01:22:08.969
the number of DTOCs in hospitals and making
sure that patients receive the appropriate

01:22:08.969 --> 01:22:13.769
care in the right locations, rather than having
people in hospital when they could be receiving

01:22:13.769 --> 01:22:17.849
more appropriate support elsewhere.

01:22:17.849 --> 01:22:27.570
We are on track to hit our commitments to
put mental health liaison teams in A&amp;Es, and

01:22:27.570 --> 01:22:32.519
there were 1.4 million calls to NHS 111 in
May.

01:22:32.519 --> 01:22:38.510
That's a nearly 8% increase on May in the
previous year, so when we think about the

01:22:38.510 --> 01:22:45.570
change in the nature of access to services,
we also need to be thinking about the changes

01:22:45.570 --> 01:22:50.920
in 111, the number of people who contact 111
are now getting clinical advice from that,

01:22:50.920 --> 01:22:54.670
the extent to which we are managing to solve
people's problems through those phone calls

01:22:54.670 --> 01:22:59.260
in order that they don't need to be handing
off to another part of the system, and I think

01:22:59.260 --> 01:23:05.800
that's a tremendous increase in our - the
set of channels through which people can access

01:23:05.800 --> 01:23:10.219
urgent advice and urgent support, alongside
our continued roll-out of urgent treatment

01:23:10.219 --> 01:23:16.550
centres where people can go to get urgent
access to primary care services.

01:23:16.550 --> 01:23:23.570
In terms of the priorities going into this
year, we have an even bigger focus this year

01:23:23.570 --> 01:23:29.630
on demand and capacity across the acute sector,
and you'll see at the back of the report the

01:23:29.630 --> 01:23:34.920
extent to which we are looking at developing
our - how do we make sure patients don't and

01:23:34.920 --> 01:23:38.810
up in hospital unnecessarily, what are our
series of offers to patients, how do we make

01:23:38.810 --> 01:23:42.559
sure that patients don't stay in hospital
longer than they need to, and how do we get

01:23:42.559 --> 01:23:44.110
them discharged to the right locations?

01:23:44.110 --> 01:23:49.689
And now a growing effort and focus on length
of stay, the patients who are in hospitals

01:23:49.689 --> 01:23:57.679
for very long periods of time, over three
weeks, and how we improve the flow-through

01:23:57.679 --> 01:24:01.579
of hospitals to get patients discharged more
quickly.

01:24:01.579 --> 01:24:09.709
Primary care, we're now, as Dominic said,
at 55% of practices offering extended access

01:24:09.709 --> 01:24:16.860
across evenings and weekends, which is ahead
of our target, and for this time, and on target

01:24:16.860 --> 01:24:22.389
to get to 100% of the country by the end of
the year.

01:24:22.389 --> 01:24:28.570
We are - and I should just touch on cancer,
as well, where we've seen the 62-day cancer

01:24:28.570 --> 01:24:34.139
performance dip in the last month, having
improved very significantly the month before.

01:24:34.139 --> 01:24:41.329
That was predicted in terms of what the cancer
alliances were expecting, but there's still

01:24:41.329 --> 01:24:46.880
an enormous amount of work to get us back
repeatedly and sustainably onto the 62-day

01:24:46.880 --> 01:24:49.080
target.

01:24:49.080 --> 01:24:54.170
Just touching on elective care, we now have
just over four million patients on the waiting

01:24:54.170 --> 01:24:57.809
list; that's a 6% increase on this time last
year.

01:24:57.809 --> 01:25:01.320
There's a lot of focus, particularly in NHS
Improvement at the moment, on the work we

01:25:01.320 --> 01:25:07.340
need to do in order to stop the waiting list
from growing, and I think that's a significant

01:25:07.340 --> 01:25:11.389
effort that's going to be needed this year
in order to turn that upward trajectory round

01:25:11.389 --> 01:25:19.639
and hold the waiting list constant before
we start to improve it again.

01:25:19.639 --> 01:25:25.219
We've talked a number of times at this Board
around - about STPs and integrated care systems,

01:25:25.219 --> 01:25:30.630
and we're pleased that we've been able to
announce the next four integrated care systems

01:25:30.630 --> 01:25:35.310
to be launched in Gloucestershire; Suffolk
and North East Essex; West, North and East

01:25:35.310 --> 01:25:41.329
Cumbria; and West Yorkshire and Harrogate,
which is a sign of the way in which systems

01:25:41.329 --> 01:25:47.430
are coming together to address challenges
in partnership, across primary care, social

01:25:47.430 --> 01:25:52.929
care, community services, mental health and
the acute sector, thinking in terms of their

01:25:52.929 --> 01:26:00.150
population needs, and I think that is proving
to be a very powerful driver of integrated

01:26:00.150 --> 01:26:02.110
change.

01:26:02.110 --> 01:26:07.550
And then just lastly, to touch back onto IT.

01:26:07.550 --> 01:26:16.189
We've now launched the first five exemplar
projects for our local health and care records,

01:26:16.189 --> 01:26:21.510
which will be London, Manchester, Wessex,
West Yorkshire and the Thames Valley area.

01:26:21.510 --> 01:26:26.829
I think those are crucially important IT projects
in terms of bringing together the information

01:26:26.829 --> 01:26:33.780
for patients for their direct care, so that
wherever they contact health or care, the

01:26:33.780 --> 01:26:37.760
information that the person who's supporting
them needs is available.

01:26:37.760 --> 01:26:43.469
So, that will include information coming from
GPs and hospitals, and community settings,

01:26:43.469 --> 01:26:49.300
and social care, in order to give us the ability
to provide care in the right place rather

01:26:49.300 --> 01:26:54.429
than patients needing to go to where the information
is, and that kind of - in the medium term,

01:26:54.429 --> 01:26:58.969
I think that is one of our key strategic steps
forward as we look at redesigning services

01:26:58.969 --> 01:26:59.969
around patients.

01:26:59.969 --> 01:27:03.150
So, a great deal going on at the moment, Malcolm.

01:27:03.150 --> 01:27:04.880
Great, well, thank you very much for that.

01:27:04.880 --> 01:27:08.789
Can we then invite any comments or questions
to Matthew?

01:27:08.789 --> 01:27:09.789
Yes?

01:27:09.789 --> 01:27:17.599
I'd just pick up in the annexe to the paper,
I wanted to just highlight the results of

01:27:17.599 --> 01:27:20.289
the impact of the vanguard programme.

01:27:20.289 --> 01:27:23.530
Can I just explain that there's been an issue
about the distribution of that page.

01:27:23.530 --> 01:27:28.460
So, it's on our public website, but it's not
up on BoardPad, unless Liz, you've been able

01:27:28.460 --> 01:27:29.599
to do it just now.

01:27:29.599 --> 01:27:33.119
But please, can you feel free to talk us through
it.

01:27:33.119 --> 01:27:43.179
Okay, so reducing emergency admissions was
the main focus of the 23 MCP and PACS vanguards.

01:27:43.179 --> 01:27:49.010
And we now have the complete time-series data
for the period of the vanguard funding.

01:27:49.010 --> 01:27:57.079
And across the duration of the programme,
between 2014/15 and 2017/18, emergency admissions

01:27:57.079 --> 01:28:04.030
grew more slowly on a per capita basis in
vanguard areas than in the non-vanguard areas.

01:28:04.030 --> 01:28:08.429
This information was, I think, first published
in the recent NAO report.

01:28:08.429 --> 01:28:16.880
It showed that it had risen over that period
by 0.9% across the MCPs, 2.6% in the PACS,

01:28:16.880 --> 01:28:22.639
and this is compared to 6.3% in the rest of
the NHS.

01:28:22.639 --> 01:28:30.420
And we can confidently say that the result
is statistically significant, bearing in mind

01:28:30.420 --> 01:28:32.219
data quality issues.

01:28:32.219 --> 01:28:40.760
We've also seen a bigger reduction in relation
to the care homes vanguard.

01:28:40.760 --> 01:28:47.429
They've held emergency admissions flat during
the period, whereas there's been an over 8%

01:28:47.429 --> 01:28:52.829
rise in admissions for care home residents
across the non-vanguard areas.

01:28:52.829 --> 01:28:58.420
And then finally, I'd like to flag that the
vanguards have reported through - and this

01:28:58.420 --> 01:29:03.719
has been through an NHS England assurance
process - that they're making very good strides

01:29:03.719 --> 01:29:07.840
in - against their return on investment plans.

01:29:07.840 --> 01:29:11.889
They're achieving 90% of what they were intending.

01:29:11.889 --> 01:29:17.269
And this equates to a return of £2 for every
£1 spent.

01:29:17.269 --> 01:29:23.530
I think we just have to commend the extraordinary
work that was done on the vanguards programme,

01:29:23.530 --> 01:29:27.079
and those results, which as you say, are not
statistically insignificant.

01:29:27.079 --> 01:29:36.579
I mean, these are I think a model of how we
anticipate a roll-out around integrated care

01:29:36.579 --> 01:29:37.579
across the country.

01:29:37.579 --> 01:29:42.269
I mean, what's particularly notable to me
is what you say about the care homes, because

01:29:42.269 --> 01:29:47.670
that's not a particularly complex model to
establish around the country.

01:29:47.670 --> 01:29:51.329
How do you see that rolling out over the next
two or three years in the broader context

01:29:51.329 --> 01:29:52.710
that we're now working in?

01:29:52.710 --> 01:29:57.869
So, we've already seen very significant spread
of the care home model.

01:29:57.869 --> 01:30:05.459
This Board took a paper a little while ago
with the 20 evidence-based components of that

01:30:05.459 --> 01:30:07.639
framework.

01:30:07.639 --> 01:30:15.189
And through the work between Matthew's team
and mine, that is being supported - wider

01:30:15.189 --> 01:30:16.909
spread of that is being supported.

01:30:16.909 --> 01:30:23.809
We have data from CCGs which shows their level
of maturity in completing aspects of that

01:30:23.809 --> 01:30:28.090
framework, and we're seeing very significant
uptake of that.

01:30:28.090 --> 01:30:35.219
I'd particularly like to highlight the importance
of two facets of that, one of which is having

01:30:35.219 --> 01:30:40.230
a single GP practice per care home, and there's
a little bit more work to be done on that.

01:30:40.230 --> 01:30:47.030
And the second is the role of clinical pharmacists
actually working directly in care homes to

01:30:47.030 --> 01:30:54.479
try and tackle things like contraindicated
polypharmacy through regular medication reviews.

01:30:54.479 --> 01:30:59.059
We have a dedicated programme we've set up
to support national spread of this, and we're

01:30:59.059 --> 01:31:05.969
directly funding over 200 pharmacists to work
in care homes specifically to solve that problem,

01:31:05.969 --> 01:31:08.099
and that I think is already having an effect.

01:31:08.099 --> 01:31:09.099
Yes.

01:31:09.099 --> 01:31:13.760
In the visits that Wendy and I, and others,
have made to care homes, that was a really

01:31:13.760 --> 01:31:20.590
critical part of reducing adverse - or actually
reducing unwarranted admissions to hospital

01:31:20.590 --> 01:31:26.070
on the grounds of adverse drug reactions by
getting to the root of the polypharmacy programme.

01:31:26.070 --> 01:31:28.179
Can I take any further - yes, Jane?

01:31:28.179 --> 01:31:33.770
I just wanted to add something to what Matthew
talked about when he was mentioning the need

01:31:33.770 --> 01:31:38.739
to reduce length of stay and our concentration
on people that have been in hospital beds

01:31:38.739 --> 01:31:39.739
for a long time.

01:31:39.739 --> 01:31:44.849
Some of the Board will be aware of the End
PJ Paralysis campaign that we've been running

01:31:44.849 --> 01:31:48.820
as a social movement, which is largely led
by nurses and allied health professionals.

01:31:48.820 --> 01:31:53.189
It's a UK-wide one, but has got multiple engagement
across England.

01:31:53.189 --> 01:31:57.860
And we started it in the middle of April,
and we've been collecting data up until the

01:31:57.860 --> 01:31:58.860
end of June.

01:31:58.860 --> 01:32:03.929
And I just thought it - I mentioned this yesterday
at the conference I was doing for nursing,

01:32:03.929 --> 01:32:05.889
celebration of nursing over the last 70 years.

01:32:05.889 --> 01:32:14.119
But we - in that time, across the UK, we had
660,000 patient days where patients were up

01:32:14.119 --> 01:32:15.119
and out of bed.

01:32:15.119 --> 01:32:17.929
And of those, 90% were dressed in their own
clothes.

01:32:17.929 --> 01:32:25.130
And that might seem - I mean, it's a great
number, but the question people might ask

01:32:25.130 --> 01:32:26.130
was, 'Well, so what?'

01:32:26.130 --> 01:32:30.610
The big difference is, we've now got a timeline
of data, and we're doing a bit more detail

01:32:30.610 --> 01:32:34.170
on it to see whether there's causation as
well as correlation, but there is a very clear

01:32:34.170 --> 01:32:40.719
showing where we see length of stay of over
seven days and over 21 days where it's beginning

01:32:40.719 --> 01:32:41.920
to reduce.

01:32:41.920 --> 01:32:46.360
And anecdotally, we're seeing reduction in
falls, reduction in pressure ulcers, reduction

01:32:46.360 --> 01:32:51.170
in patient complaints, and we're hearing quite
a lot of stories of people going home earlier

01:32:51.170 --> 01:32:57.440
because they feel better, they look better,
and we've had evidence of people saying, "Well,

01:32:57.440 --> 01:33:01.429
it's a Saturday morning and you're up and
dressed, would you like to go home?", rather

01:33:01.429 --> 01:33:02.659
than waiting till the Monday.

01:33:02.659 --> 01:33:04.269
So, we need to do more data analysis.

01:33:04.269 --> 01:33:08.539
We're waiting for - we're going to keep the
data collection going until the end of this

01:33:08.539 --> 01:33:09.539
month.

01:33:09.539 --> 01:33:14.459
We're also looking with colleagues that have
set this up to actually track things like

01:33:14.459 --> 01:33:16.709
falls and pressure ulcers.

01:33:16.709 --> 01:33:23.889
But it would appear that this - what is a
relatively straightforward piece of work,

01:33:23.889 --> 01:33:28.579
is actually having a significant impact on
the length of stay of patients in hospital,

01:33:28.579 --> 01:33:30.429
and that's got to be good news.

01:33:30.429 --> 01:33:34.300
So, I just thought it was something that was
a really constructive, positive thing, and,

01:33:34.300 --> 01:33:38.610
you know, on behalf of me and the rest of
- I would like to thank all of those staff

01:33:38.610 --> 01:33:44.689
that actually tried something new and have
really led this with huge dedication and commitment.

01:33:44.689 --> 01:33:50.229
Massive impact, and the pictures of patients
in their own clothes, smiling, feeling better;

01:33:50.229 --> 01:33:51.229
some of the quotes we've had have been phenomenal.

01:33:51.229 --> 01:33:54.469
I mean, it is deeply impressive.

01:33:54.469 --> 01:33:58.820
Those of us who have seen some of this, it's
quite an extraordinary change in culture within

01:33:58.820 --> 01:34:00.189
the hospital setting.

01:34:00.189 --> 01:34:01.550
It's been brilliant.

01:34:01.550 --> 01:34:02.550
Absolutely brilliant.

01:34:02.550 --> 01:34:03.550
Yes.

01:34:03.550 --> 01:34:07.190
Right, are there any further issues on Matthew's
report?

01:34:07.190 --> 01:34:12.099
And by the way, I've now noticed that your
vanguard appendix is up on BoardPad, so we

01:34:12.099 --> 01:34:15.860
can look at the data in our own time.

01:34:15.860 --> 01:34:17.440
Okay, I think we're done.

01:34:17.440 --> 01:34:18.710
Thank you very much on that.

01:34:18.710 --> 01:34:24.659
Paul, may we move on now to the consolidated
