01:34:32.090 --> 01:34:35.199
Well, you can do month two, shall we cut out
the one.

01:34:35.199 --> 01:34:37.409
Yes, month two would be a better thing to
talk about.

01:34:37.409 --> 01:34:42.019
So, in the report you have in front of you,
we've set out in paragraphs two and three

01:34:42.019 --> 01:34:48.030
the position on the commissioner plans, which
have been developed for 18/19, the expenditure

01:34:48.030 --> 01:34:53.880
profile across the various types of activity,
and the good news is that the commissioner

01:34:53.880 --> 01:34:57.389
plans, as they stand, have a balanced position
at the bottom of them, which was clearly what

01:34:57.389 --> 01:35:00.760
we are committed to deliver for the year.

01:35:00.760 --> 01:35:06.959
As you look as this report and just comparing
to last year, we need to remember that the

01:35:06.959 --> 01:35:11.110
complication of the system reserve, which
we had last year in looking at any of these

01:35:11.110 --> 01:35:17.130
numbers, does not exist in 18/19, as all of
that money has been deployed upfront to the

01:35:17.130 --> 01:35:18.130
front line.

01:35:18.130 --> 01:35:23.599
So, in 18/19, reporting for the commissioning
sector is very much a case of what you see

01:35:23.599 --> 01:35:28.880
is what you get, rather than, -This is what
we report and there's a separate reserve somewhere

01:35:28.880 --> 01:35:29.880
else.

01:35:29.880 --> 01:35:32.739
That's just really important, I think, in
contextualising what we then say about what

01:35:32.739 --> 01:35:35.199
the numbers tell us.

01:35:35.199 --> 01:35:40.920
Turning to the financial position as at month
two, as is customary at this early stage of

01:35:40.920 --> 01:35:45.900
the year, there's little if any variance in
CCG or indeed direct commissioning numbers,

01:35:45.900 --> 01:35:50.510
and I wouldn't actually read too much at this
stage into the small variances that you can

01:35:50.510 --> 01:35:54.570
see on central costs and technical items.

01:35:54.570 --> 01:35:59.139
Of potentially greater significance is the
risk assessment, which I've set out at the

01:35:59.139 --> 01:36:04.840
back end of the report, which obviously comes
from the process we run alongside the core

01:36:04.840 --> 01:36:06.810
forecast month in, month out.

01:36:06.810 --> 01:36:13.340
This is currently showing net risks in the
CCG sector of £325 million which, as I've

01:36:13.340 --> 01:36:19.599
pointed out in the report, compares with £443
million at this stage last year.

01:36:19.599 --> 01:36:25.840
And there is also a risk of £84 million in
NHS England's commissioning budgets.

01:36:25.840 --> 01:36:31.619
As you would expect, we are working intensively
with the affected organisations to manage

01:36:31.619 --> 01:36:37.979
these risks, and I don't perhaps need to say
to the Board that we remain committed to delivering

01:36:37.979 --> 01:36:42.349
financial balance overall as a consequence
of that work.

01:36:42.349 --> 01:36:45.459
At this stage, that's about as much as I can
say.

01:36:45.459 --> 01:36:49.540
I would promise a slightly more interesting
and exciting report possibly in the right

01:36:49.540 --> 01:36:53.530
way in the next meeting in September, Malcolm.

01:36:53.530 --> 01:36:54.530
Thank you very much, Paul.

01:36:54.530 --> 01:36:56.330
Not too exciting, I hope.

01:36:56.330 --> 01:37:04.499
What we look to you for is bland assurance,
and underpinned by great intelligence.

01:37:04.499 --> 01:37:06.949
So, we look forward.

01:37:06.949 --> 01:37:08.840
But thank you for the assurance on this occasion.

01:37:08.840 --> 01:37:11.599
Are there any questions to Paul?

01:37:11.599 --> 01:37:14.170
No, well, thank you very much.

01:37:14.170 --> 01:37:15.590
You've passed the test.

